\par\par \b \uc2 RBA\uc2  Likely to Talk About Geopolitical Risk Next Week -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2022\par 03:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2117 ET - The \uc2 RBA\uc2  may mention growing geopolitical risk and market instability in its policy meeting statement on Tuesday, but in reality, it was already sidelined by data earlier this week showing wages growth remained largely benign in 4Q. ANZ says Ukraine will get a mention in the statement, with the \uc2 RBA\uc2  possibly talking about how the impact might be felt in Australia through higher commodity prices, while adding there will be offsetting impacts on sentiment because of uncertainty, financial market volatility and higher petrol prices. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1941 ET - Russia's invasion of Ukraine will likely have global central banks recalibrating their policy outlooks, says \uc2 Franklin Templeton\uc2  . Markets are entering uncharted territory in terms of the implications of a war on Europe's borders, it says. The conflict between Ukraine and Russia could draw in other countries and \uc2 NATO\uc2  , or the war could be short, but the occupation long, it adds. In the coming days and weeks, investors will be weighing a range of topics, including the implications for hard and soft commodities, and whether the current global inflationary wave will give way to deflation, as global demand weakens in a prolonged conflict scenario, FT adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1806 ET - Strong Australian wages growth will continue even after the \uc2 RBA\uc2  raises official interest rates multiple times, says ANZ. The experience from the pre-GFC period is that high wages growth can be persistent, even in the face of aggressive \uc2 RBA\uc2  rate hikes. ANZ adds that current policy settings are on track to generate a material acceleration in wages growth. After a decade of weak wages growth, restrictive policy settings will eventually be required to bring wages growth down to a level consistent with achieving the 2%-3% inflation target over time, it says. This is likely to be a cash rate of 3% or more. The official cash rate now stands at 0.10%. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1749 ET - Municipal bond prices reversed weeks of decline Thursday as investors flocked to haven assets following the Russian attack on Ukraine. Benchmark ten-year triple-A general obligation muni bonds yields fell to 1.57% Thursday from 1.65% Wednesday, according to Refinitiv MMD, declining alongside Treasurys. Yields on more speculative triple-B munis also dropped to 2.28% from 2.36%. Yields fall as prices rise. (heather.gillers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1649 ET - One Bank of Mexico board member considered the Feb. 10 interest-rate decision to be a dilemma between a 50bp and a 75bp hike, although minutes to the meeting give no indication the larger increase was seriously considered as the board voted 4-1 to lift the overnight rate target by 50bp to 6%, with a dissenting member favoring 25bps. The hawkish member noted that other central banks have raised rates in triple digits and intend to maintain such a tightening pace. The dissenting member said the bank shouldn't get too far ahead of the Fed in raising rates. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1648 ET - Mexican stocks recover opening losses prompted by Russia's invasion of Ukraine and close up 0.2% at 51454 points. Mining shares gain with Grupo Mexico up 4.1% and Pe\u241\'00\'F1oles shares rising 2.2%. \uc2 Cemex\uc2  shares close down 1.1%, adding to yesterday's losses. \uc2 Cemex\uc2  said it activated its $500M share buyback program yesterday purchasing around 10M CPO shares for about $5.2M. The peso slid to 20.56 against the US dollar from 20.24 yesterday. In local economic data, core inflation rose to a more than 20-year high 6.52% in mid-February from 6.21% in January, and December retail sales were down 0.4% from November but 5.2% above year-earlier levels. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1607 ET - The Ukraine-driven dollar rally stabilizes, with the WSJ US Dollar index rising nearly 1% to 90.36, off its intraday high of 90.90. The greenback gains around 1% versus the euro and the pound and 0.5% versus the yen. Elliot Wave's Murray Gunn tells WSJ the geopolitical tension favors the USD. "Given that the vast majority of global debt is denominated in US dollars, periods of 'risk-off' like this generally result in a scramble for dollars." He adds the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also a potential save haven "given Japan's domestic macroeconomic bias." Gunn expects the yen to strengthen for the time being. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1547 ET - The 10-year Treasury yield closes little changed, at 1.969%, after sliding in the morning on news that Russia attacked Ukraine, while the 30-year manages to rise 0.017 percentage point to 2.291%. Investors wonder how the conflict might alter US monetary policy. "With the Fed already being perceived as 'behind the curve,' we doubt these events will stay the Fed's hand next month," \uc2 BMO\uc2  says. But given the economic risks and volatility, the war "will at the very least, likely temper some of the most aggressive calls on the Fed," such as a 50 basis point rate increase, according to \uc2 BMO\uc2  . (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1351 ET - The number of newly listed homes for sale during the four weeks ending Feb. 20 was down just 2% year over year, says \uc2 Redfin\uc2  , for the smallest decline since mid-November. Pending sales rose 1%, the first increase since mid-January. The market again set new record highs for home sale prices, asking prices, buyers' mortgage payments and the share of homes selling within days of hitting the market. There was also a new all-time low for the total number of homes for sale. The good news, \uc2 Redfin\uc2  says, is that each week more homes are being listed for sale.(chris.wack@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1330 ET - \uc2 Gildan Activewear\uc2  's "blowout" 4Q, according to \uc2 CIBC\uc2  , was driven by strong consumer demand and the benefits from its Back To Basics strategy. Net income totaled $173.9 million, or 89 cents per share, up from $67.4 million, or 34 cents per share, last year. The company's new three-year growth strategy outlines the next phase of growth "as it looks to deliver organic sales growth off an improved cost profile," says \uc2 CIBC\uc2  . With strong demand in North America, the company reported an EBIT margin of 20.2%, surpassing its aspiration of 18%. \uc2 CIBC\uc2  says that Gildan is built on strong fundamentals and is well-positioned to grow its share across end-markets . \uc2 CIBC\uc2  increases its target price on the stock to $49 from $47.(adriano.marchese@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1220 ET - The Russian ruble's depreciation in reaction to the Ukraine crisis is likely to fuel expectations that \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise interest rates aggressively in coming months, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "USD/RUB may be trading below its early morning highs just below the 90.00 area, but the RUB is the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world this year," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. USD/RUB rises 7.4% to 87.3706, having earlier reached a record high of 89.9900, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . The ruble has pared some of its earlier losses after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and market participants are likely betting on further rate rises to bolster the ruble, Foley says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1218 ET - The Swedish krona is the worst performing G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday due to Sweden's sensitivity to Russia's military invasion of Ukraine, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "Neither Sweden nor Finland are members of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and their geographical position around the Baltic sea ensures that whether or not they should be in \uc2 NATO\uc2  is a topical and sensitive discussion," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "The SEK could remain under pressure as long as Russia remains on the offensive." EUR/SEK rises 0.5% to 10.6737 after hitting a 21-month high of 10.7570 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2022 21:17 ET (02:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220225ei2p0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
