\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Moscow recovers 22% and the ruble rises after tepid U.S. sanctions\b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Russian stock market rebounded after Thursday's 33% plunge in the face of the sanctions imposed by the United States. Yes, Biden has imposed limitations on the import of technology, blockades to large banks and punishments to oligarchs close to Putin. But he has tiptoed around the energy issue and has not removed Russia from the SWIFT system, which allows transfers in dollars and euros to the country. Thus, after being closed for several minutes, the Russian market rebounded around 20%.\par \par \uc2 The banks, having learned that the harsher punishment that would have resulted from the expulsion from the international transfer system will not materialize, at least for the time being, with TSC Group up 25%. The ruble-denominated MOEX index is up 20%.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the ruble gained 2.3% against the dollar to 83.30 united, after touching a record low of 89.60 on a volatile day on Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported by the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2014, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  annexed Crimea from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The market expects more action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has to address inflationary risks. The weakening of the ruble following the invasion of Ukraine is expected to dent living standards in Russia and stoke inflation, which is already approaching 9%. Interest rates in the country stand at 9.5%, after rates were raised by one percentage point in the middle of this month.\par \par \uc2 Russia could now carry out an unplanned rate hike as it did in late 2014, when it raised its key interest rate to 17% from 10.5% late in the evening amid the ruble's slide, analysts say. "We think a one-off hike of 400 basis points or more by the of 400 basis points or more by the BCR is likely, with the key rate above 13%," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  explains in a note.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220225ei2p004ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
