\par\par \b \uc2 Invasion Presents Big Risks For Putin -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2022\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Russian president has sought for decades to recapture the Soviet Union's lost status \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Ann M. Simmons and Matthew Luxmoore \f28 \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW -- Russia's invasion of Ukraine is President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's most aggressive move yet in his two-decade-long campaign to reassert Russia's place in the world and redeem the humiliation of the fall of the Soviet Union.\par \par \uc2 It is an extraordinary gamble.\par \par \uc2 In invading his smaller neighbor, Mr. Putin now faces a breakdown in Moscow's ties with the West, which will slap sanctions on Russia aimed at inflicting deep pain on its economy.\par \par \uc2 Members of the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2 , which have already sent fresh military equipment and forces to its eastern members in recent weeks, have found new unity in deterring Russia.\par \par \uc2 "He's gave priority to his own obsession over Russia's interests," said Kadri Liik, a senior policy fellow at the \uc2 European Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 . "But he risks Russia's economic development, Russia's global position and also Russia's internal stability in order to get hold of Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Putin, in power since 1999, now runs the risk of a prolonged conflict in Ukraine that could be akin to the Soviet Union's disastrous 1980s war in Afghanistan -- something that could ultimately stir dissent at home.\par \par \uc2 The "risk of getting stuck for a long time" wouldn't sit well with most Russians, said Pavel Salin, an independent political analyst based in Moscow. "The situation inside the country could turn against him."\par \par \uc2 The invasion quickly reverberated inside Russia on Thursday. Russian banks experienced a run on deposits, while the Russian stock market plummeted. The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the ruble.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Putin has laid the groundwork for his decision for years, with demands for a wholesale revision to the trans-Atlantic security arrangements that emerged from Russia's defeat in the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 "Judging by [his] actions, he considers the risk justified," Mr. Salin said. Mr. Putin likely believes that "this quick action will force the West to accept a new reality. These are his calculations. How much they correspond to reality, how they will be justified, time will tell."\par \par \uc2 The military campaign marks the continuation of a policy that has seen Mr. Putin steadily expanding Russia's sphere of influence, reasserting Moscow's dominion over former Soviet republics such as Belarus, Georgia and Moldova.\par \par \uc2 But in doing so, he is sacrificing his relations with the West and risks making Russia a pariah. That could in turn leave Moscow more reliant on China, a more powerful neighbor with whom Russia already has an unequal relationship.\par \par \uc2 "Russia will upend its old relationship with the West, it'll now turn openly confrontational," Ms. Liik said. "And that will leave Moscow both politically and economically much more firmly in China's orbit. And I wonder if that is something that Moscow actually wants."\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the U.S. and Europe were preparing far-reaching sanctions that follow a first round of penalties leveled this week for Mr. Putin's recognition of two separatist, Russian-controlled regions in Ukraine's east. The earlier sanctions targeted Russian sovereign debt, banks and wealthy Russians linked to Mr. Putin's inner circle.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Putin told a meeting Thursday with heads of Russia's leading businesses that the military campaign was "a necessary measure" and said "we were simply left with no chance to act otherwise."\par \par \uc2 He projected calm as he told the gathering that "Russia remains a part of the world economy and, in this regard, as much as it remains a part of it, we are not planning to damage the system in which we ourselves feel ourselves a part," he said. "It seems to me that our partners should understand this and not set themselves the task of pushing us out of this system."\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin has sought to protect its economy against sanctions, building a buffer against economic retaliation by the U.S. and its allies. After the West imposed sanctions on Russian individuals and entities following Moscow's 2014 annexation of Crimea and its incursion into eastern Ukraine soon after, Russia began building a cushion of reserves.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has $630 billion in reserves of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold, having grown from $368 billion in 2015, when billions of dollars were spent supporting the ruble after the West imposed sanctions in response to Crimea.\par \par \uc2 Still, jitters over the events rippled Thursday in \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday to prop up the ruble after it plunged to a record low in the hours after troops invaded Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Following an early morning address by Mr. Putin announcing the start of a "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Ukraine, Russians lined up at ATMs to withdraw cash amid fears the government would restrict hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Putin has steadily tightened his grip over Russian society, which could help him withstand any domestic opposition to the Ukraine invasion.\par \par \uc2 He enjoyed a burst of popular support when Russia annexed Crimea. And while his popularity had slipped somewhat in recent years on concerns over the economy, a poll taken before the invasion by the independent Levada Center pollster found Mr. Putin's approval rating at 69%.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, crowds of Russians demonstrated at rallies across the country. Authorities detained more than 850 people, according to protest monitor OVD-Info. On Moscow's Pushkin Square, several hundred people gathered with signs reading "No to War!" In St. Petersburg, Russia's second-largest city, some 200 people chanted antiwar slogans.\par \par \uc2 But room for dissent has generally been squeezed in Russia after authorities over the past year dismantled the opposition, closed top rights groups and forced several independent media to close.\par \par \uc2 "The elites are under control, the disaffected part of the population, which right now could oppose the military scenario, don't have the infrastructure with which they can mobilize. And the majority is under the influence of propaganda," Mr. Salin said.\par \par \uc2 Leading opposition politician \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 , who in recent years had organized the largest street protests against Mr. Putin, was jailed in February 2021.\par \par \uc2 A popular opposition blogger, Arseny Bobrovsky, said in an interview before the Ukraine invasion that he warned his fellow Russians about the steady buildup of Russian troops around Ukraine over the past few months to little avail. "It's like you're watching a guy put on diving equipment but instead of thinking he's now going to go diving, you say, 'Oh, he's just going to go for a walk,'" the 38-year-old said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Russian state media has offered a full-throated defense of the Kremlin's aggression against Ukraine, claiming that Russia was goaded into pursuing the military campaign.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the country's communications regulator, \uc2 Roskomnadzor\uc2 , threatened heavy fines and blocking websites of media that published information about the campaign not citing official Russian sources\par \par \uc2 "There will be difficulties," said Andrey Veselov, a supporter of Mr. Putin who owns a store selling European-made gardening equipment, speaking before the Ukraine invasion. "But we won't fall apart."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Veselov's store imports German-made Stihl chain saws and landscaping equipment, and he said that if \uc2 EU\uc2  businesses sever ties he can switch to buying Japanese and Chinese-made equipment.\par \par \uc2 He praises the import substitution campaign Russia launched following sanctions imposed over its annexation of Crimea in 2014, and said Mr. Putin is right to defend Russia's interests in the face of Western pressure. "We have to stand our ground," he said.\par \par \uc2 --Evan Gershkovich contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ann M. Simmons at ann.simmons@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (February 25, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 25, 2022 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220225ei2p000lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
