\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 [Live] "Missile attacks on Kiev," official officials report.\b0\par\par\uc2 8,640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 A night view of Kiev after the mayor declared a curfew between 10 p. m. and 7 a. m. on February 24, 2022. In the early hours of the 25th, loud explosions were reported near the Ukrainian capital.Photo: Pierre CromInrecent hours, tensions around Ukraine rose after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered "military action" in the Donbas. Western leaders, such as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  and \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , condemned what happened, while warning that they will take action in response to what they consider "an unjustified attack". Meanwhile, explosions are reported from several Ukrainian cities and the country warns that it was attacked by Russian troops from Belarus. We tell you, minute by minute, what is happening in these tensions\par \par \uc2 .???????????? Before we begin, a bit of context: for weeks, Russia has deployed some 150,000 troops on the border with Ukraine and on Monday recognized the independence of two separatist territories: Donetsk and Lugansk. This stoked accusations that the Kremlin is preparing a large-scale attack against the neighboring country\par \par \uc2 .???????????? And a summary of what happened in the week:\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Germany suspended authorization of \uc2 Nord Stream 2.On\uc2  the same day, the Russian Senate authorized sending soldiers to separatist enclaves: The vote formally allowed Putin to send troops to the Donbas region of Ukraine.At the same time, the United States mobilized more troops to Eastern Europe.On Wednesday, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  formally announced sanctions on Russian leaders, as well as on banks and the defense sector. While Ukraine (prior to the Russian military action) announced a plan to mobilize its reservists and urged its citizens to leave Russia.Biden also announced sanctions against Russia on Wednesday: This first round of sanctions will prevent Russia from raising Western funds to repay sovereign debt. The US also said it would sanction the company in charge of operating the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline.Yesterday, Ukraine declared a state of national emergency, while neighboring countries are preparing for a possible migration wave. About 5 million people could be displaced, according to U.S. data.Russia also said on Wednesday that separatist regions in eastern Ukraine asked it for military backing. Hours later, Reuters reported that five explosions were heard in the city of Donetsk, while the U.S. said a full-scale invasion was imminent.10:40 p.m.: Border unit in Zaporizhia region targeted by missile attackTheUkrainian border post in Zaporizhzhya region was hit by a missile attack, the Ukrainian Border Guard Service reported. The attack, according to the announcement, occurred at 4:25 a. m. (Ukrainian local time) at the Primorsky Pasad border unit in the Zaporizhia region. According to reports, there are dead and wounded.\par \par \uc2 10:33 p.m.: Videos show explosion south of KievAlarge explosion occurred in the sky over the southern outskirts of Kiev, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports. Witnesses filmed burning debris falling over parts of the city. The videos appeared to show at least two surface-to-air missiles fired from the Ukrainian capital before the explosion.\par \par \uc2 10:14 p.m.: Russian plane shot down over KievTheUkrainian Interior Ministry said a Russian plane was shot down over Kiev. As read in The Guardian, \uc2 Anton Gerashchenko\uc2  , an adviser to Ukraine's interior minister, added a series of photos to his Telegram account, including what he said was a nine-story residential building on fire.\par \par \uc2 10:10 p.m.: Chinese embassy in Ukraine seeks to evacuate its citizensThediplomatic entity is organizing charter flights for Chinese citizens seeking to evacuate. There are about 6,000 Chinese citizens in Ukraine. The announcement came after a \uc2 Chinese Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman refused to call Russia's attack on Ukraine an "invasion," reports \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 9:57 p.m.: Loud explosions heard in Ukraine's capitalTwoloud explosions rang out early Friday (local Ukrainian time) in central Kiev on the second day of Russia's invasion of Ukraine, an AFP reporter noted\par \par \uc2 ."Attacks on Kiev with cruise or ballistic missiles resumed. I heard two loud explosions," Ukrainian Deputy Interior Minister Anton Herashchenko said on his Telegram account.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine's armed forces confirmed that additional weapons are being brought to Kiev, amid reports of explosions in the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 The press that is in Ukraine also reported that the explosions were heard. This was reported by \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  reporter Emma Graham Harrison.\par \par \uc2 9:00 p.m.: The \uc2 European Union\uc2  announces the approval of another package of "massive and targeted sanctions" against RussiaThiswasannounced by the President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 , who assured, via \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , that "the approved package shows how united the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is". This package aims to affect the following areas:\par \par \uc2 Financial sanctions, targeting 70% of the Russian banking market and key state-owned enterprises, including in defense.Energy sector, a key economic area that particularly benefits the Russian state. The export ban will affect the oil sector, making it impossible for Russia to upgrade its refineries.Aviation, as the sale of aircraft and equipment to Russian airlines is prohibited. In addition, Russia's access to crucial technology, such as semiconductors or state-of-the-art software, is being limited.Visas: diplomats and related groups and businessmen will no longer have privileged access to the \uc2 European Union\uc2 ."8:18 p. m.: New Zealand, Russia and Japan announce sanctions againstRussiaAustralia\par \par \uc2 's Prime Minister \uc2 Scott Morrison\uc2  said he will impose more sanctions on the Russians. "Australia will impose further sanctions on the oligarchs, whose economic clout is of strategic importance in masking members of the Russian parliament who voted to authorize the use of Russian troops in Ukraine to invade it. We are also working with the United States to align with its new sanctions on television commercials, which are key accomplices in the aggressions."\par \par \uc2 For her part, New Zealand Prime Minister \uc2 Jacinda Ardern\uc2  called for the immediate withdrawal of Russian forces in Ukraine, announced a travel ban for Russian officials and added that she is considering expelling the Russian ambassador. "New Zealand calls on Russia to do the right thing and immediately cease military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine, and withdraw permanently to avoid catastrophic and pointless loss of innocent lives," she said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Japan said it plans to limit semiconductor exports to Russia, while freezing the assets of some individuals and groups linked to Russia, and will stop issuing visas to certain Russians.\par \par \uc2 8:00 p.m.: Russian forces close in on Kiev, Ukrainian capitalAccording toThe \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , by the end of the first day of the invasion, Russian special forces and airborne troops were advancing toward the outskirts of Kiev. While it is not known exactly what Putin's ultimate goal is, U.S. officials believe that it may be to impose the replacement of President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 's government. Meanwhile, Ukrainians continue to move toward the western side of the country.\par \par \uc2 6:50 p.m.: WhiteHouse White Housepress secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  told a news conference that there are credible reports that personnel\par \par \uc2 at theChernobylfacility are being held hostage.\par \par \uc2 This illegal and dangerous hostage-taking, which could disrupt the routine civil service efforts needed to maintain and protect the waste facilities nu6\par \par \uc2 :00 p. .m: Volodimir Zelenski, president of Ukraine, gives a report on those killed in the Russian invasion"137 heroes, our citizens," lost their lives, Zelenski said in a video posted on the government's website, adding that 316 other Ukrainians were injured during the clashes\par \par \uc2 .In his remarks, he added: "I don't see anyone" to fight alongside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 5:39 p.m.: Ukraine decrees general mobilization over Russia'sinvasionThrough the official account of the General Staff of the Armed Forces of Ukraine it was made known that in connection "with the military aggression of the Russian Federation against Ukraine, and in order to ensure the defense of the state, maintain combat readiness and mobilization of the Armed Forces of Ukraine and other military formations, a general mobilization is announced."\par \par \uc2 The decree published on the website of Volodimir Zelenski, president of Ukraine, says the measure will affect persons subject to "compulsory military service and reservists" and will be in effect for 90 days in all regions of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 5:00 p.m.: Ukrainians take shelter, as aggressions continueTheGuardian reports that aggressions continue in Ukraine. For example, it is known that throughout the night (local time) a battle took place in the Ukrainian city of Sumy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, images of residents taking shelter in Kiev and Kharkiv metro stations are circulating on social networks, as reported by Beno\u238\'00\'EEt Vitkine, a reporter for the French newspaper Le Monde.\par \par \uc2 4:30 p.m.: \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  to vote on condemnation of RussiaTheUN resolution, which is in the hands of the 15-member Security Council, would condemn, "in the strongest possible terms, Russia's aggression, invasion and violation of Ukraine's sovereignty," a U.S. official said. It also "reaffirms Ukraine's sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity. And it requires the Russian Federation to withdraw its forces immediately, completely and unconditionally." A European diplomat at the \uc2 UN\uc2  also said the resolution would declare that "Russia can no longer encroach on its neighbors."\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reads that the resolution is likely to have more symbolic, than real, power, but the U.S. and its allies have said that the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and the council have a responsibility to act, and that a Russian veto would only demonstrate the country's isolation.\par \par \uc2 4:15 p.m.: \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  talks with Vladimir PutinTheFrench president spoke with his Russian counterpart, the Kremlin reported. The two had "a serious and frank exchange" on Ukraine, and Putin "gave a thorough explanation of the reasons and circumstances" surrounding the invasion, the statement issued from Moscow read.\par \par \uc2 "After speaking with the Ukrainian president, and in coordination with him, Macron called \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to demand an immediate end to the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the French leader's office reported. In that dialogue, Macron reminded Putin that Russia was now exposed to "massive sanctions" by the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Macron is the fourth leader with whom \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has met after the attack on Ukraine, as in previous hours the Russian leader held meetings with the Prime Minister of Pakistan and with the leaders of Iran and India.\par \par \uc2 4:00 p.m.: \uc2 UN\uc2  warns of Ukrainians fleeing after invasionAbout100,000 people fled their homes in Ukraine and thousands sought refuge abroad after the Russian invasion, the \uc2 UN\uc2  said. "We think about 100,000 people fled their homes already and may have been displaced inside the country and that several thousand crossed international borders," Shabia Mantoo, a spokesman for the \uc2 UN High Commissioner for Refugees\uc2  (\uc2 UNHCR\uc2 ), told AFP.\par \par \uc2 3:47 p.m.: Explosions and a preliminary toll of wounded and dead reported from Ukraine"The Ukrainian city of Mariupol is under heavy fire with reports of hundreds of explosions. The city is one of the largest Ukrainian ports on the Sea of Azov. Taking Mariupol would help Russia secure a direct land route to Crimea, the Ukrainian peninsula annexed by Russia in 2014," so reports Kyiv Post on social media.\par \par \uc2 Journalists also reported additional explosions from the direction of Ukraine's Hostomel airport. "Big explosion from the direction of Hostomel airport just now. Bigger than the previous ones. One explosion, not several," reported reporter Jane Lytvynenko.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian Health Minister Oleh Lyashko reported that 57 people were killed and 169 injured. In addition, the deputy defense minister added that heavy shelling is still going on in the eastern Donetsk region.\par \par \uc2 3:28 p.m.: \uc2 European Union\uc2  warns of "massive and severe" sanctions against RussiaTheleaders of the 27 countries of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  agreed that "sanctions will cover Russia's financial, energy and transport sectors", according to the Declaration issued after the summit held in Brussels. The measures, which are not yet known in detail, contemplate export controls, visa issuance and the inclusion of Russian officials on the sanctioned list.\par \par \uc2 3:00 p.m.: \uc2 IMF\uc2  and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  warn about the consequences of the war in UkraineClarifyingthat they will offer assistance to Ukraine, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  (\uc2 WB\uc2 ) warned about the repercussions that the Russian aggression in Ukraine may have on the global economy\par \par \uc2 .IMF\uc2  head Kristalina Georgieva said she was "deeply concerned" about the consequences of the fighting in Ukraine and warned in a tweet that the conflict "adds significant economic risk for the region and the world." As read by AFP, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is in the process of disbursing $2.2 billion in assistance to Ukraine under a loan program that will end in June.\par \par \uc2 For his part, David Malpass, president of the \uc2 WB\uc2 , noted that "the institution "is preparing options for major support to the people of Ukraine and the region, including immediate budget support."\par \par \uc2 2:52 p. m.: The \uc2 Inter-American Court of Human Rights\uc2  speaks of the central role of international lawThisis the call that the entity made from social networks: "The role of public international law is central and today's events demonstrate it. I make a special call to solve this conflict based on the law and the application of basic principles, such as that a State must respect the sovereignty of another."\par \par \uc2 2:00 p.m.: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ends his official statement after Russian aggression in UkraineAssertingthat what is happening today is the greatest threat to Europe and the world, and reaffirming that Ukraine has been an independent country for thirty years, the U.S. president said that the sanctions interposed by the allies show the union of almost two thirds of the countries in the world. "We are not doing this alone. We represent half of the world's economy."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. president also added that, in coordination with his allies, "we will limit Russian business in dollars, pounds and euros. We are attacking their finance, technology and industrial capabilities. The sanctions, he said, are intended to have "severe consequences" on the Russian economy in the long term.\par \par \uc2 According to Reuters, Biden assured that Putin would be a pariah on the international stage and that his actions would cost Russian civilians dearly.\par \par \uc2 Biden added: "Putin has much bigger ambitions than Ukraine. He wants, in fact, to re-establish the old Soviet Union. That's what this is all about and I think his ambitions are completely contrary to where the rest of the world has come."\par \par \uc2 1:31 p.m.: Germany says Putin "will try to wipe Ukraine off the map "\uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , Germany's chancellor, said that "we are witnessing the start of a war of a magnitude that we have not seen in Europe in more than 75 years" since the end of World War II. "This is an attempt to change with the use of force the borders in Europe, perhaps even to wipe an entire country off the map."\par \par \uc2 12:52 p.m.: Russians call first day of invasion a "success"All \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  assigned to the groups of troops of the Armed Forces of the Russian Federation for the day were successfully completed," said Defense Ministry spokesman Igor Konashenkov\par \par \uc2 .Thus, Russia said its military achieved its objectives on the first day of the invasion of Ukraine, despite warnings of harsh sanctions from Western countries.\par \par \uc2 12:35 p.m.: Russian forces reportedly seized Chernobyl"After heavy fighting in the Chernobyl area, Russian troops took control of the nuclear power plant in\par \par \uc2 the\par \par \uc2 area.trying to take Chernobyl. Our soldiers are sacrificing their lives so that the tragedy of 1986 will not happen again. This is a declaration of war against the whole of Europe".12:15 p. ...: Ukraine announced a toll of dead and wounded in the south of the country.At least 13 Ukrainian civilians and nine Ukrainian servicemen were killed on Thursday in the Kherson region (southern Ukraine), which is currently under partial control of Russian troops who invaded the country, regional authorities reported.\par \par \uc2 "Thirteen civilians, including two children, were killed and nine soldiers were killed in the attack on the Kherson region," the local government specified on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . The town of Guenichesk was seized by the Russians, it added.\par \par \uc2 12:10 p.m.: \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  unveils new package of sanctions against RussiaThe prime minister said the UK is announcing the biggest sanctions package Russia has ever seen, which includes the following measures:\par \par \uc2 Russian banks will be excluded from the UK financial system.Russian state-owned and private companies will be banned from raising money in the UK.The amount of money Russians can hold in British bank accounts will be limited.Sanctions will also apply to Belarus.Aeroflot will be banned in the UK.Export controls, similar to those imposed by the United States, will be implemented, meaning a ban on items such as semiconductors and other high-tech equipment.The export of dual-use items to Russia, which could eventually be used militarily, will also be banned.Before the House of Commons, Johnson described Putin as a "bloodied aggressor who believes in imperial conquest, always determined to attack his neighbor, no matter what we did." He added that "Putin will stand condemned in the eyes of the world and in the eyes of history. He will never be able to wipe the blood of Ukraine from his hands."\par \par \uc2 12:00 p.m.: Pakistan Prime Minister Imran Khan meets Putin.He is the first leader to meet with the Russian president after the attack on Ukraine. Khan arrived in Moscow yesterday on a two-day trip, amid rising tensions between the two countries, but when the military action had not yet taken place.\par \par \uc2 Some read this visit as "ill-timed". As read in The Guardian, Michael Kugelman of the Wilson Center in the United States said: "There is a risk that Khan's visit to Moscow will be perceived by many in the West as an indirect endorsement of Putin's decree on eastern Ukraine, a move that much of the world has condemned and that Pakistan itself is unlikely to support. Although Khan's visit was planned weeks ago and focuses on bilateral relations, the timing is striking and presents some problems of optics."\par \par \uc2 11:42 a.m.: \uc2 G7\uc2  ready to act if invasion causes "disruption" to energy supplies"We are closely monitoring global oil and gas market conditions," the \uc2 G7\uc2  said in a statement following a video conference to discuss the crisis sparked by the invasion.\par \par \uc2 The group said it supports "consistent and constructive engagements and coordination between major energy producers and consumers in the collective interest of a stable global energy supply."\par \par \uc2 11:27 a.m.: 'This is a threat to the global order': G7The \uc2 G7\uc2 , which groups advanced economies, on Thursday condemned the Russian invasion of Ukraine and assured that the attack constitutes "a serious threat to the international order, with ramifications beyond Europe." Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  "has placed himself on the wrong side of history," the \uc2 G7\uc2  said in a statement following a meeting to discuss the crisis.\par \par \uc2 11:22 a.m.: Russian forces seize a military airport near Kiev.Russian forces seized Thursday a military airport about forty kilometers from Kiev, the capital of Ukraine, said Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 , who assured that the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recover it was already underway.\par \par \uc2 According to Zelenski, who was speaking in a video address, the incursion of Russian paratroopers in Gostomel, where the airport is located, was "blocked" and the Ukrainian forces "received the order to annihilate them".\par \par \uc2 10:20 a.m.: Curfew declared in KievKiev Mayor Vitaly Klitschko announced Thursday a curfew to protect the safety of the capital's residents. "The curfew will last from 10 p.m. to 7:00 a.m.\u8243\'20\'33, he added in a statement, specifying that public transport will not operate at those times but that metro stations will remain open to serve as a shelter in case of attacks.\par \par \uc2 10:09 a.m.: Fighting at Ukraine's Chernobyl power stationUkrainian authorities reported fighting near the nuclear waste repository.\par \par \uc2 According to an advisor to the \uc2 Ukrainian Interior Ministry\uc2 , Anton Guerashtshenko, the "occupants' troops entered from Belarus into the area of the Chernobyl power plant." "Members of the National Guard protecting the depot put up stubborn resistance," he said on the Telegram messaging network.\par \par \uc2 ???????? 9:55 a.m.: Biden to meet with G7.U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will brief the United States on the situation in Ukraine and next steps. According to him, this crisis will cause a "catastrophic loss of lives".\par \par \uc2 In a few moments, they will hold a virtual meeting with the leaders of the \uc2 G7\uc2  (United Kingdom, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States). Prior to the appointment, Biden "convened a meeting of the National Security Council (...) to address the latest developments in Ukraine," the White House reported.\par \par \uc2 "The United States and its allies and partners will respond in a united and determined manner. The world will hold Russia accountable," Biden said in his first, after Russian missiles began falling on Ukraine. Biden also spoke by phone with his Ukrainian counterpart, Volodimir Zelenski, and said he had pledged to "provide support and assistance to Ukraine and the Ukrainian people."\par \par \uc2 9:28 a.m.: Fighting erupts at military base near KievThe Ukrainian military announced Thursday that there was fighting with Russian troops near Kiev for control of the Gostomel military airport.\par \par \uc2 "There is ongoing fighting for control of the Gostomel airport," located a few kilometers northwest of Kiev, Valery Zaloujnyi, speaking on behalf of the Ukrainian armed forces, announced in a message on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . AFP reporters saw helicopters flying at low altitude towards the city from the north.\par \par \uc2 8:46 a.m.: Russian authorities threaten those taking part in protests.The \uc2 Russian Interior Ministry\uc2 , the Prosecutor's Office and the \uc2 Russian Investigative Committee\uc2  warned their population against any protest actions. The Committee noted that people who take part in rallies about "the tense situation in foreign policy" or in clashes face prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Journalist Kevin Rothrock tweeted some images of the protests in Moscow. "They are arrested almost immediately, often as they leave their homes," Rothrock wrote.\par \par \uc2 8:32 a.m.: Four missiles were fired from Belarus.The Armed Forces of Ukraine reported that four ballistic missiles were fired from the territory of Belarus in the southwestern direction.\par \par \uc2 7:53 a.m.: 'Russia is invading on multiple fronts': former Ukrainian ministerAndriy Zagorodnyuk, Ukraine's former defense minister, told \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  that there are ground invasions on several fronts in the country. "They are quite close to the city of Kherson now. The situation is really critical there. They are also trying to surround Kharkiv. It seems that Kharkiv at the moment is the main target. They are also trying to get to Kiev using a battlefield group. The group left Belarus and was stopped," Zagorodnyuk commented.\par \par \uc2 ???????? 7:33 a.m.: Ukrainian military plane crashes near KievA Ukrainian military plane crashed Thursday near Kiev with 14 people on board, the state emergency situations service announced. The plane went down near the village of Trypillia, about 50 kilometers south of the Ukrainian capital, according to the same source.\par \par \uc2 ???????? 6:55 a.m. 6:55 a.m. Russian forces arrive in the Kiev region: border guardsRussian troops crossed the Vilcha border point in the Kiev region, 50 kilometers from the border with Belarus, the Ukrainian Border Guard Service reported today. "Russian troops violated the state border. The enemy's war equipment crossed through the Vilcha border point, about 150 kilometers north of the capital," the entity reported according to which the Ukrainian Army planted battle with the Russian Army.\par \par \uc2 6:39 a.m.: \uc2 NATO\uc2  activates defense plansThe \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  countries agreed to activate their defense plans, following Russia's offensive in Ukraine, announced Thursday the secretary general of the military alliance, \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 . During a press conference in Brussels, the official also confirmed that the transatlantic military alliance will hold a leaders' summit on Friday via videoconference in the wake of the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 6:29 a.m.: Multiple explosions were heard in Kiev."Confirmed by Ukrainian authorities. A large air assault \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Mi-8 helicopters at Antonov International Airport in Hostomel. Interior Ministry says Russia has taken control. Very dangerous; it's just 15 minutes west of a highway from the capital," wrote \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  correspondent Christioher Miller.\par \par \uc2 6:10 a.m.: Ukraine asks Turkey to close Russian access to the BosphorusUkraine asked Turkey to close the Bosphorus straits to Russian warships, which use the passages to enter the Black Sea, \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  reported. "We conveyed our official request to the Turkish side regarding the closure of the airspace, the Dardanelles and of the Bosporus for Russian ships," Ukraine's ambassador to Ankara, Vasyl Bodnar, said, according to Turkish media Hurriyet. Bodnar added that Ukraine is also asking Turkey to sanction Russian businesses.\par \par \uc2 ???? 5:30 a.m.: \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies invoke Article 4."Today we have held consultations under Article 4 of the Washington Treaty. We have decided, in accordance with our defensive planning to protect all Allies, to take additional measures to further strengthen deterrence and defense throughout the Alliance. Our measures are and will remain pre-emptive, proportionate, but not escalatory," the North Atlantic Council said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 4:59 a.m.: Separatists in eastern Ukraine are taking control of territories: RussiaThe Russian military announced Thursday that pro-Russian separatists in eastern Ukraine were making territorial gains against the Ukrainian army.\par \par \uc2 General Igor Konashenkov, a spokesman for the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2 , said in television remarks that the rebels advanced three kilometers in the Donetsk region and one and a half kilometers in Lugansk, and assured that civilians had "nothing to fear" because Russia has military targets.\par \par \uc2 4:46 a.m.: "At least five Ukrainian servicemen die in Russian invasion": Ukrainian governmentThe official number of Ukrainian servicemen killed in the Russian "military action" that began on Thursday rose to five, the Interior Ministry said. One air defense serviceman died in the Zaporiyia region (south), while four border guards were killed in several localities of the Kherson region (south), the ministry said.\par \par \uc2 ???????????? 4:40 a.m.: Ukraine breaks off diplomatic relations with Russia."We have severed diplomatic relations with Russia. For all those who have not yet lost their conscience in Russia, it is time to come out to protest against the war with Ukraine," President \uc2 Vladimir Zelensky\uc2  maintained on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 4:17 a.m.: Ruble plunges to record lowsRussia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bonds and stocks plunged, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years, Reuters reported. "To stabilize the situation on the financial market, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to initiate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," the bank said Thursday on its \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 4:16 a.m.: Ukraine claims 50 Russians killed on eastern front.Ukraine's military announced Thursday that at least 50 Russian occupants in the Lugansk region were killed in the first hours "of the Russian invasion of the country."\par \par \uc2 "On February 24, about 50 Russian occupants were liquidated near the locality of Shchastia in eastern Ukraine," the army's General Staff said in a statement. Ukrainians use the term "Russian occupier" both to refer to a Russian soldier and to pro-Russian separatist fighters in the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 2:24 a.m.: \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , figurehead of diplomacy in this conflict, speaks out.The president, who wanted to assume the role of mediator between Russia and Ukraine, assured that "France strongly condemns Russia's decision to declare war on Ukraine. Russia must put an end to its military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately. France stands in solidarity with Ukraine . It supports the Ukrainians and works with its partners and allies to end the war."\par \par \uc2 2:00 a.m.: Japanese prime minister speaks on repercussions of Russian attack on world order.Fumio Kishida said that "the Russian invasion shakes the foundations of the international order, which does not allow unilateral attacks to upset the status quo. We strongly condemn Russia. We will coordinate efforts with the international community and react quickly."\par \par \uc2 1:46 a.m.: \uc2 UN\uc2  chief asks Putin to stop conflict in the name of "humanity."As read by AFP, at the end of the emergency meeting of the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 , which coincided with Putin's announcement of the start of a military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of his troops in Ukraine, \uc2 Antonio Guterres\uc2 , \uc2 UN\uc2  secretary general, said that it is "the saddest day" he has had at the head of the organization.\par \par \uc2 "President Putin, in the name of humanity, bring the troops back to Russia. In the name of humanity, don't let what could be the worst war since the turn of the century start in Europe. Stop it now," he implored.\par \par \uc2 1:15 a.m.: Ukraine reports that five Russian planes and a helicopter were shot down.Via \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , the \uc2 Ukrainian Defense Ministry\uc2  reported that it shot down five Russian aircraft and one helicopter. The message reads, "According to the Joint Forces Command, today, February 24, five planes and one helicopter of the aggressor were shot down. The Joint Forces give a worthy rebuff to the Armed Forces of the Russian Federation. The military units are in their positions. The enemy is suffering losses. Keep calm and believe in the Ukrainian defenders - together we will win, glory to Ukraine!".\par \par \uc2 Now, the Russian military denied that one of its aircraft was shot down in the fighting over Ukraine during the morning. Meanwhile, as read by AFP, Russia claimed it destroyed Ukrainian air bases and anti-aircraft defense.\par \par \uc2 1:04 a.m.: Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski imposes martial law in the country.Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski imposed martial law in his country after Russia launched its military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country. Although he did not go into details, he suggests that military forces will have extraordinary powers in this situation of extreme alarm.\par \par \uc2 12:40 a.m.: Ukraine says it was attacked from Belarus."At around 5:00 a.m., Ukraine's state border in the area with the Russian Federation and the Republic of Belarus was attacked by Russian troops supported by Belarus," Ukraine's state border service said.\par \par \uc2 The entity added that attacks are being carried out in Luhansk, Sumy, Kharkiv, Chernihiv and Zhytomyr regions, areas on the eastern and northern borders of Ukraine. The attacks are targeting border units, border patrols and checkpoints with artillery, heavy