le," he added.\par \par \uc2 ???????? Thursday, February 24 ????????12:00 a.m.: \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  is issuing a speech.Zelenskiy said Russia has carried out missile strikes against Ukraine's infrastructure and Ukrainian border guards, with explosions heard in many cities across the country. He added that he spoke to the U.S. president and told Ukrainians to stay at home and remain calm: "The military is working. I will be with you all the time. Stay strong. We will win because we are Ukraine. Glory to Ukraine." According to Reuters, the prime minister assured that he will introduce martial law in all territories of the state.\par \par \uc2 11:45 p.m.: Ukrainian officials warn that Russian troops are landing in Odessa and Mariupol.Ukrainian officials say Russian troops have landed in Odessa while others are crossing the border into Kharkiv. \uc2 Ukraine's Interior Ministry\uc2  made the announcement on Telegram and added that the rocket attacks are targeting Ukrainian fighter jets at an airport outside Kiev. Ukraine's state emergency service says attacks were launched against 10 Ukrainian regions, mainly in the east and south of the country.\par \par \uc2 11:12 p.m.: Ukraine confirms Russia's "full-scale invasion".Ukrainian Foreign Minister \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  confirmed the Russian "full-scale invasion" of Ukraine. "Putin has just launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine. Peaceful Ukrainian cities are on strike. This is a war of aggression. Ukraine will fight back and win. The world can and must stop Putin. The time to act is now."\par \par \uc2 This pronouncement was echoed by Ukrainian Deputy Interior Minister \uc2 Anton Gerashchenko\uc2 , who said via \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that "the invasion has begun. There have just been missiles at the military headquarters, airports, military warehouses, near Kyiv, Kharkiv, Dnieper. The shooting at the border is underway. As of this day, there is a new geopolitical reality in the world."\par \par \uc2 11:03 p.m.: At the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 , Ukraine and Russia exchange messages.Russia's representative to the \uc2 United Nations\uc2 , \uc2 Vasily Nebenzya\uc2 , justified Russia's military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Article 51 of the \uc2 UN\uc2  charter, which allows for "self-defense." The Ukrainian ambassador to the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  responded that the Russian president declared "a war against my country."\par \par \uc2 10:53 p.m.: "Putin chose a premeditated war": Biden on military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in UkraineU.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was quick to react to the "military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ordered by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in eastern Ukraine. According to the President, the Russian military forces have launched an "unprovoked and unjustified" attack which is being suffered by the Ukrainian people. He went on to say that with \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies he would "coordinate a strong and united response to deter any aggression against the alliance".\par \par \uc2 In addition, Biden assured that he will monitor the situation and that early tomorrow morning he will meet with his \uc2 G7\uc2  counterparts. He will then address the American people to announce the "future consequences that the United States and its allies will impose on Russia for this unnecessary act of aggression against Ukraine and global peace and security."\par \par \uc2 10:37 p.m.: Reports of explosions in Ukrainian cities after Putin's announcementShortly before the president's announcement, some Ukrainian cities began reporting explosions.\par \par \uc2 Around 4:00 a.m. Ukrainian time (9 p.m. Colombian time), explosions were reported in the city of Mariupol, in the east of the country.Around 5:00 a. m. (10 p. m. Colombian time), reports came from Kramatorsk.The explosions in Kramotorsk followed reports of bombs in Kharkiv, the country's second largest city.Explosions were also reported in Kiev, according to CNN's team in the area, but no further information has been received on this case.Odessa, a city in the east of the country, was the last city to report explosions.We will continue to report on reports of more explosions on Ukrainian territory as more information comes in.\par \par \uc2 10:00 p.m.: Putin announced "military action" in DonbasAccording to information read in \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 , in an address to the Russian people now taking place at the same time as the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  meeting is taking place, the Russian president said that:\par \par \uc2 Clashes between Ukrainian and Russian forces are "inevitable" and it was "only a matter of time."Further expansion of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and its use of Ukrainian territory is unacceptable.The Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at "protecting people".Circumstances "demand decisive action by Russia."Putin is still speaking and what is known from his speech is that he wants to "demilitarize and denazify" Ukraine. He further warned that "in case of foreign interference, Russia will act immediately" and that the responsibility for the bloodshed will be in the hands of the "Ukrainian regime."\par \par \uc2 9:44 p.m.: \uc2 Antonio Guterres\uc2  spoke at the ongoing meeting of the \uc2 UN Security Council.At\uc2  the emergency meeting being advanced by the \uc2 UN\uc2  body, the Secretary General said, "If in fact an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being prepared, I have only one thing to say: Putin stop your troops from attacking Ukraine. Give peace a chance, too many people have already died."\par \par \uc2 In addition, Albania's representative, Ferit Hoxha, called the situation "senseless madness." "Russian wars have nothing to do with your security. This is a confrontation between Russia and international law," he added.\par \par \uc2 9:20 p.m.: Ukrainian airspace closed.Flights of civilian aircraft through Ukrainian airspace were suspended after a notice to airmen (Notam) was issued. Earlier, airports in the cities of Dnipro, Kharkiv and Zaporizhzhia were closed.\par \par \uc2 8:05 p. m.: Blinken expects 'full invasion' before the night is overU.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said he expects Russia to invade Ukraine before the end of the night [Eastern time], but still sees the possibility of "avoiding major aggression." Speaking to NBC News, he asserted, "Everything seems to be in place for Russia to engage in major aggression against Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Blinken also claimed to ABC News that Russia is "putting the finishing touches" on having forces in place for an "all-out invasion" and said the U.S. "has certainly seen the presence of Russian forces inside Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 7:37 p.m.: Five explosions reported in eastern UkraineAt least five explosions were heard in the city of Donetsk, a witness told Reuters. According to what is read in \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 , after the explosions, four military trucks could be seen driving to the site. Earlier, the Kremlin said that two separatist regions in Ukraine had asked for Russia's help in responding to the Ukrainian army's "aggression."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the United States said a full-scale invasion is imminent and that Russian troops and separatist forces surrounding the country are in combat-ready positions. Pentagon spokesman John F Kirby assured reporters that "Russian forces continue to gather closer to the border. They are ready to go. They could strike at any time with significant military force."\par \par \uc2 7:20 p.m.: Press reports new cyber attack in Ukraine.Ukrainian media report that at least six government websites are down, given an alleged cyber attack. "This attack also hit the websites of the ministries of Infrastructure, \uc2 Strategic Industries\uc2  and Education," the Kyiv Independent newspaper reported. According to the publication, the websites of the Cabinet of Ministers, the ministries of Foreign Affairs and Agriculture, as well as that of the security service are down.\par \par \uc2 6:56 p.m.: \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  said that Russia had approved an offensive against Ukraine.The Ukrainian president finished a 10-minute speech in which he also assured that his Russian counterpart, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , had not responded to his invitation for talks. This was reported by Reuters when translating the speech Zelenskiy gave in Ukrainian and Russian.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president also warned that any outburst from Russia could "burn everything" and create a catastrophic crisis. Speaking in Russian, he asked Russians not to believe the narratives about Ukraine presented on the neighboring country's state television.\par \par \uc2 "I started a phone call with the president of the Russian Federation. Result: silence. This step could be the beginning of a big war on the European continent. The whole world is talking about what could happen at any moment. Any provocation. Any outburst, one that could burn everything."\par \par \uc2 5:50 p. .m.: Russian mercenaries are seeping into separatist-controlled region, European officials sayEuropean officials claimed that about 300 mercenaries, from the Russian paramilitary group Wagner, arrived in Donetsk and Lugansk. Intelligence services tracked them from Libya and Syria until they reached Crimea, from where they left for the separatist regions, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reads.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine requested a meeting with the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 5:00 p.m.: Ukrainian soldiers prepare in case of a Russian attack.According to reports by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Ukrainian troops stationed in separatist areas in the east of the country are ready in case a battle with Russia breaks out. In Zaitseve, in the Russian-backed separatist region of Lugansk, soldiers held their positions amid explosions felt on Wednesday. \uc2 Oleksiy Danilov\uc2 , head of Ukraine's National Defense and Security Council, said, "Our army is ready" to defend against an attack.\par \par \uc2 3:50 p.m.: Leaders of separatist regions ask Russia for help.According to information from Ifax, citing Russian government spokesman Dmitry Peskov, the leaders of two separatist regions in eastern Ukraine have asked Russia for help in the face of aggression by the Ukrainian army. Many experts point to these requests as the latest (possibly final) pretext Russia could use to launch an all-out attack on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 As read in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the Kremlin said Putin had received a request for "help in repelling aggression by Ukrainian armed forces and formations." Russian media have claimed that Ukraine is about to launch an offensive against Russian-backed separatist territories in eastern Ukraine, something the Kiev government denies it intends to do.\par \par \uc2 2:55 p.m.: Ukraine imposes a state of national emergency.Ukraine's parliament has just voted to approve a state of emergency, which will begin on February 24 and last for 30 days, with the possibility of extension. According to Ukrainian press reports, such as Kyiv Independent, the state of emergency may provide for:\par \par \uc2 increased protection and security of public ordercontrols of civilian identification documentsban on proteststemporary or permanent evacuation of people from dangerous areas and their accommodationprohibition of relocation of conscripts and reservists without prior noticeprohibition of producing and disseminating information that could "destabilize the situation".prohibition of amateur radio transmitting devices.It may also include:\par \par \uc2 curfewa special entry and exit regimeban on mass events"special rules" for the dissemination of online information.2:53 p.m.: Neighboring countries and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  prepare for a possible wave of migrantsSeveral European countries, along with stationed U.S. military personnel, are preparing to house more than 1 million refugees from Ukraine if Russia continues the invasion, the Washington Post reports.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Ambassador to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . \uc2 Linda Thomas-Greenfield\uc2  warned that the conflict could spark "a new refugee crisis" with "as many as 5 million" displaced people.\par \par \uc2 2:40 p.m.: Pentagon says Russian troops are prepared for an attack that could be imminentAs read in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the Pentagon said that 80% of the 190,000 Russian troops and separatist forces in or near Ukraine are now in combat-ready positions, and that a full-scale military assault is very likely imminent. "They are ready to go now if they get the order to do so," a senior Defense Department official told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials say Russian forces have been preparing for months for a large-scale attack and are now in a position to carry it out. That attack would likely include missile salvoes, air strikes, special forces \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an amphibious assault and ground troops, and could begin at any time, one official told the U.S. newspaper.\par \par \uc2 2:00 p.m.: Ukrainian conflict death toll risesThe town of Schastia, located on the line of contact with the separatist Luhansk People's Republic in Ukrainian-controlled territory, has been without power and water for several days after artillery cut off water and electricity supplies. Lines of people gather at ATMs and at the borders.\par \par \uc2 ???????????? 1:20 p. m.: Sanctions against \uc2 Nord Stream 2The\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration announced economic sanctions against the company building the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  gas pipeline. Why is this project so important to Putin?\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin needed approval from German regulators for this pipeline which, if \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would deliver 55 billion cubic meters of gas per year directly from Russia to \uc2 Europe.Gazprom\uc2 , the Russian state-owned company that owns the project, said its average export price was $280 per 1,000 cubic meters of gas in 2021, meaning the new pipeline could be worth more than $15 billion a year.The issue is key for the region, as with natural gas prices near an all-time high, many fear that further tension will result in serious repercussions to European consumers."The pipeline would allow Russia to send gas to Europe without going through Ukraine, meaning Moscow could put pressure on Kiev without the risk of Kiev cutting off the gas supply route in retaliation. Ukraine has lobbied furiously against the project, saying it undermines its national security," explained several Guardian reporters.1:18 p.m.: EU prepares to launch second package of sanctions against RussiaThe European Union warned that it is ready to issue more sanctions against Russia if that country's troops move beyond Ukraine's separatist regions. "If there are further Russian aggressions and further incursions into Ukrainian territory, we are ready to step up our response also in terms of sanctions," \uc2 European Commission\uc2  executive vice president \uc2 Valdis Dombrovskis\uc2  told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 12:58 p.m.: "Russia moved nearly 100% of its troops; readies for invasion": U.S. official."Russia has moved nearly 100 percent of forces to the location needed for an invasion, according to a senior U.S. defense official," Jack Detsch, a journalist for Foreign Policy, wrote on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 11:51 a.m.: "An invasion could displace 5 million people": US.U.S..A Russian invasion of Ukraine could generate a new refugee crisis, with up to five million people displaced, the U.S. ambassador to the Assam warned Wednesday????????????\par \par \uc2 11:03 a. m.: Ukraine suffers new cyberattackUkrainetoday suffered a new cyberattack, the third in a few weeks, against several banks and government and parliamentary websites, Digital Transformation Minister Mykhailo Fedorov reported today. "At approximately 4 p. m. (local time) began another massive DDoS (denial of service) attack on our state," he wrote on his official account on the Telegram social network.\par \par \uc2 11:00 a.m.: EU formally announces sanctions against RussiaTheCouncil of the European Union (EU), which brings together member states, on Wednesday formally adopted EU sanctions against Russia for recognizing the independence of Donetsk and Lugansk, which include a ban on senior Russian officials traveling to European territory, limit Moscow's access to European financial markets and contain a trade embargo on the separatist regions\par \par \uc2 .10:34 a.m.: Ukrainian soldier diesAUkrainian soldier was killed Wednesday in shelling near the front line with pro-Russian separatist territories in the east, the Armed Forces reported. The statement did not specify the location of the attack, merely noting that the soldier "suffered fatal wounds" and that another serviceman was wounded.\par \par \uc2 10:22 a.m.: EU convenes special summitThursdayEuropean Council President \uc2 Charles Michel\uc2  called for a special \uc2 European Union\uc2  summit in Brussels on Thursday to discuss the crisis between Ukraine and Russia, according to a letter released Wednesday\par \par \uc2 ."Russia's aggressive actions violate international law, and the territorial integrity and sovereignty of Ukraine. They also undermine European security," Michel said in the letter to the 27 heads of state and government invited to the summit.\par \par \uc2 9:00 a.m.: UK to send more weapons toUkraineBritish Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announced Wednesday that the UK will send more weapons to Ukraine for its defense. "In light of Russia's increasingly threatening behavior, and in line with our previous support, the UK will shortly provide an additional package of military support to Ukraine," Johnson said at Prime Minister's Questions in Parliament.\par \par \uc2 8:35 a.m.: Russia evacuates diplomatic staff from UkraineRussiaannounced Tuesday its intention to evacuate its diplomats from Ukraine, accusing local authorities of not doing enough to ensure their safety. This Wednesday, the Russian flag was no longer flying on the roof of the diplomatic building and several people were seen leaving the site with suitcases.\par \par \uc2 8:21 a.m.: Russia promises response after Western sanctions"Let there be no doubt: there will be a strong response to these sanctions, not necessarily symmetrical, but well-calculated and painful for the United States," the \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  said in a statement\par \par \uc2 .6:52 a.m.: \uc2 European Union\uc2  to adopt Russia sanctions todayDatafrom \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  shows that, if approved, the \uc2 EU\uc2  will impose travel bans and freeze the assets of 23 senior Russian officials. The bloc would also sanction 351 members of the Russian State Duma who voted in favor of recognizing the two territories. "\uc2 EU\uc2  officials are also drawing up measures to ban trade between the two Russian-controlled territories in eastern Ukraine," reads the British newspaper publication\par \par \uc2 .???????????? 6:02 a.m.: Ukraine readies for nationwide state of emergencyTheNational Security and Defense Council of Ukraine (NSCD) decided today to institute a state of emergency throughout the country in the face of Russian aggression, except in the Donetsk and Lugansk regions, which are already under a politico-military administration. The decision must now be ratified by the Verkhovna Rada, or Ukrainian Parliament, CSND Secretary Oleksiy Danylov told a press conference. "I will report it to the Rada today," Danilov said.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to this mechanism, the official explained, the authorities will be able to increase the protection of public order and strategic infrastructure, "limit traffic, intensify the verification of vehicles and documents of individuals," Danilov said. "These are preventive measures to preserve calm in the country, so that the economy continues to function," he added. The implementation of the state of emergency will not mostly affect the lives of Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 5:10 a.m.: Pope Francis speaks out on Ukraine"Despite the diplomatic efforts of recent weeks, we have before us an increasingly worrying situation," Francis said, following his weekly audience. "I ask all the parties involved to refrain from actions that could provoke even more suffering among the people," the pope added.\par \par \uc2 "I have great sorrow in my heart for the situation in Ukraine," the pope confessed, two days after Russia's recognition of the independence of pro-Russian separatist territories in Ukraine, raising fears of military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 4:49 a.m.: Ukraine announces mobilization of its reservistsTheUkrainian armed forces announced a plan to mobilize reservists starting Wednesday, due to fears of a military escalation with Russia. The mobilization covers reservists aged 18 to 60 for a maximum term of one year, the Ukrainian army specified on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Ukraine has about 200,000 reservists and 250,000 active military personnel in the armed\par \par \uc2 forces.???????????? 3:40 a.m.: Ukraine asks its citizens to leave RussiaTheUkrainian Foreign Ministry urged its citizens to leave Russia quickly, given the risk of a military offensive by Moscow. "The ministry recommends Ukrainian citizens to refrain from traveling to Russia and those who are already in Russia to immediately leave the territory," the ministry said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 In developmentWith\par \par \uc2 information from Efe and Afp*????????????\par \par \uc2 Have you already heard about the latest news in the world? We invite you to check them out at El Espectador.\par \par \uc2 Receive alerts from GoogleNewsRelated topicsNewsthe worldThe world todayLatest news in the worldNews todayLatest newsCrisis in UkraineRussiaUkrai