\par\par \b \uc2 Pound Likely to Extend Losses Vs Dollar on Russia-Ukraine Crisis -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par 11:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1028 GMT - Sterling should weaken further versus the dollar after Russia launched an unprecedented invasion of Ukraine, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. The market had already lowered its U.K. interest rate rise bets in recent days after comments from \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  officials and could further rein in those expectations in response to the conflict, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a note. "The scaling back of more aggressive BOE rate hike expectations in the near-term combined with more risk-off trading conditions should see cable [GBP/USD] correct lower and test year to date lows at 1.3358 in the coming weeks," he says, noting the dollar's safe haven status. GBP/USD falls 0.9% to a three-and-a-half-week low of 1.3419, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT - U.S. consumer product firm \uc2 BellRing Brands\uc2  ' high-yield bond offering could be a bellwether for risk sentiment in credit markets, says \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  . The firm is expected to sell around $840 million in 10-year senior notes non callable on the first five years Thursday, as it is looking to finance its spin-off from Post Holding. "Demand for this issuance will be key to understand whether appetite for junk remains intact despite the escalation in Ukraine, and a spike in inflationary expectations," it says. Russia's attack on Ukraine Thursday has prompted investors to flight to safety, as they sell risk assets such as stocks and buy perceived haven government bonds. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1012 GMT - European stocks lose ground as Russia's military assault on Ukraine spooks investors. The Stoxx Europe 600, DAX and CAC 40 fall more than 3% and the FTSE 100 is down 2.7%. Brent crude surges 7% to $100.77 a barrel and gold and silver prices rise 2% and 3%, respectively as traders flock to safe havens. "Shares continued to tumble globally Thursday, with all European sectors down as investors fled riskier assets amid worsening military conflict in Ukraine," ActivTrades says. "It's now harder and harder for investors to predict where markets are going to go next as many expect the U.S. and its allies to proceed with strong retaliation measures in the shape of deeper economic sanctions." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1011 GMT - Safe-haven assets such as German Bunds, the U.S. dollar and gold are likely to remain in demand until there's more clarity regarding the escalating situation in Ukraine, Donner & Reuschel says. "The decisive factors now are how long the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  will last, how far Russian troops will advance into Ukraine and what the reactions will be from the West and China," Carsten Mumm, chief economist of the bank, says. The situation is still too unclear to take a new position, he says, adding that in the past, armed conflicts have usually only resulted in short-term setbacks on stock markets. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT - Investors looking to boost their portfolios should brace for strength in the U.S. dollar, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says, after Russian missiles and airstrikes hit Ukraine's capital, Kyiv, and more than a dozen other cities across the country. The Swiss bank notes the U.S. dollar is a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that tends to rally during periods of heightened geopolitical uncertainty. "We think that market expectations of six or seven U.S. interest rate hikes are likely to support the US dollar in the months ahead. As such, we see the U.S. dollar as an attractive tactical \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position at present," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. (mauro.orru@wsj.com; @MauroOrru94)\par \par \uc2 0952 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is underestimating inflation risks, says \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  . Governor Andrew Bailey pushed back market pricing of interest-rate increases Wednesday by telling lawmakers on the House of Commons' Treasury Committee that markets shouldn't get carried away. "We still think the BOE remains way behind the curve on U.K. inflation risks, particularly over the medium-term," \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  analysts say, adding that dovish comments risk damaging their credibility further. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0951 GMT - Investors should keep calm and build a robust portfolio to navigate the Ukraine crisis and rising U.S. interest rates, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says, after Russian missiles and airstrikes hit Ukraine's capital, Kyiv, and more than a dozen other cities across the country. Investors should diversify their portfolios across regions, sectors, and asset classes, use commodities as a geopolitical hedge and brace for strength in the U.S. dollar, \uc2 UBS\uc2  notes. "While it is impossible to judge the precise magnitude of geopolitical effects on markets, such events have generally not prevented equities from moving higher over a medium-term horizon, and drawdowns driven by geopolitical stress events are typically short-lived for well-diversified portfolios," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. (mauro.orru@wsj.com; @MauroOrru94)\par \par \uc2 0951 GMT - Malaysia's inflation risks seem to remain tilted to the upside despite the consumer-price index moderating for the second straight month to 2.3% in January, \uc2 UOB\uc2  says. Among key upside risks include global supply-chain disruptions, escalating commodity prices, higher wages due to post-pandemic labor shortages and a revised national minimum wage, \uc2 UOB\uc2  says. The moderation in CPI for January was purely a result of high base effects, partially cushioned by higher prices in most goods and services, particularly food and beverages, the bank adds. \uc2 UOB\uc2  maintains its 2022 full-year inflation forecast at 3.0%. (chester.tay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0948 GMT - The possibility that the escalation of the Russia-Ukraine conflict sends inflation to new heights adds to the case for additional protection and a prudent approach to risk assets, \uc2 Amundi\uc2  says. The asset manager prefers European equities with a value tilt and is cautious on the highest growth areas of the market. \uc2 Amundi\uc2  has reduced its exposure to credit risk, in particular, to euro-denominated investment-grade bonds, and added hedges, it says. "The idea that the local crisis could morph into a global situation suggests that we should add protection regarding U.S. equity markets as well as U.S. credit high yield," \uc2 Amundi\uc2  says. (maitane.sardon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0941 GMT - Investors should reconsider credit risk, says \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  . While high-yield corporate bonds have outperformed investment-grade bonds since the beginning of the year, there are signs that credit risk might start to crumble, the Copenhagen-based bank says, adding that no junk bond has been issued since Feb. 10 on heightened market volatility. Also, leverage loan indexes are falling on both sides of the Atlantic and real rates rising, it says. Risk assets come under renewed pressure Thursday as Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, with the pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 down 2.8%. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT - European bank stocks trade sharply lower after Russia attacks Ukraine, prompting Western countries to prepare broad sanctions against Moscow. \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank International\uc2  leads the decline with a 14% drop. The Vienna-listed bank made 33% of its pretax profit in Russia last year and is by far the most exposed out of large listed European banks. Its shares have lost 30% of their value in the past week amid escalating conflict in the region. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  and \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  , which made about 6% and 4% of their pretax profit in Russia according to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  analysts, fall 6.8% and 7.1%, respectively. Major European banks also trade deep in the red, with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  falling 6%, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  6.4% lower and \uc2 UBS\uc2  down 5.8%. (cristina.roca@wsj.com; @_cristinaroca)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - The Russian ruble faces further significant losses even as \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilize market conditions, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday said it would start \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the ruble hit a record low after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched an invasion of Ukraine. Market participants already expected the bank would need to raise interest rates aggressively before the conflict, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a note. "It is likely that ruble volatility will remain at more extreme levels with the ruble at risk of further sharp sell offs in the days and weeks ahead." USD/RUB rises 5.5% to 85.1960 after hitting a record high of 89.9900 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2022 05:28 ET (10:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220224ei2o001b8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
