\par\par \b \uc2 Gildan Activewear Strategy is Paying Off -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par 19:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1330 ET - \uc2 Gildan Activewear\uc2  's "blowout" 4Q, according to \uc2 CIBC\uc2  , was driven by strong consumer demand and the benefits from its Back To Basics strategy. Net income totaled $173.9 million, or 89 cents per share, up from $67.4 million, or 34 cents per share, last year. The company's new three-year growth strategy outlines the next phase of growth "as it looks to deliver organic sales growth off an improved cost profile," says \uc2 CIBC\uc2  . With strong demand in North America, the company reported an EBIT margin of 20.2%, surpassing its aspiration of 18%. \uc2 CIBC\uc2  says that Gildan is built on strong fundamentals and is well-positioned to grow its share across end-markets . \uc2 CIBC\uc2  increases its target price on the stock to $49 from $47.(adriano.marchese@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1220 ET - The Russian ruble's depreciation in reaction to the Ukraine crisis is likely to fuel expectations that \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise interest rates aggressively in coming months, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "USD/RUB may be trading below its early morning highs just below the 90.00 area, but the RUB is the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world this year," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. USD/RUB rises 7.4% to 87.3706, having earlier reached a record high of 89.9900, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . The ruble has pared some of its earlier losses after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and market participants are likely betting on further rate rises to bolster the ruble, Foley says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1218 ET - The Swedish krona is the worst performing G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday due to Sweden's sensitivity to Russia's military invasion of Ukraine, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "Neither Sweden nor Finland are members of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and their geographical position around the Baltic sea ensures that whether or not they should be in \uc2 NATO\uc2  is a topical and sensitive discussion," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "The SEK could remain under pressure as long as Russia remains on the offensive." EUR/SEK rises 0.5% to 10.6737 after hitting a 21-month high of 10.7570 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1201 ET - The Ukraine conflict adds to the tailwind already boosting the US dollar, says Nancy Lazar, from \uc2 Piper Sandler\uc2 . "Prior to the invasion, we were expecting a stronger dollar on the back of the [global] slowdown we've been expecting," she says, adding that now the "Russia/Ukraine war will put even more upward pressure on the dollar." The greenback strengthens against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the euro and the pound, and the WSJ US Dollar Index rises 1.1%. Longer term, the USD/EUR is up 9% in 12 months. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1153 ET - It is unlikely that Brussels will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help curb the price of carbon, Jefferies says. Carbon prices in the \uc2 EU\uc2  's Emissions Trading Scheme have surged over the last few weeks to more than EUR90 a metric ton, data from \uc2 Intercontinental Exchange\uc2  shows. This presents challenges for industrial companies and consumers at a time of rising energy costs, Jefferies says, noting that some diplomats met on Monday to discuss measures to curb prices. The likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains low, as the bloc won't take steps that could be perceived as watering down its climate ambitions, Jefferies says. The measures could lead to much higher prices in the late 2020s when deeper emissions reductions are necessary to meet the bloc's goals, it adds. (maitane.sardon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1152 ET - Large, late inventory orders are increasingly weighing on corporate balance sheets. Logistics and finance experts say extended shipping delays are tying up importers' cash as they rush to restock depleted inventories, pushing businesses to borrow money and seek other forms of financing. Commercial banking services provider \uc2 US Bancorp\uc2  says its supply-chain finance business balances doubled YoY. Freight forwarder Flexport says its inventory-financing loans more than doubled from 3Q to 4Q last year, and COO Sanne Manders says companies' liquidity needs "are going through the roof" as ocean shipping delays mount. (lydia.oneal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1136 ET - Due to the latest events in the Russia-Ukraine conflict, Oxford Economics expects higher European gas, oil and food prices over the medium term. The economic research firm also expects more financial market disruption and tougher \uc2 European Union\uc2  and U.S. sanctions on Russia. "Higher inflation is the main transmission channel for a weaker economic outlook," Oxford Economics says. The conflict and all its spillover effects leads Oxford Economics to cut its global GDP growth forecast by 0.2 percentage points in 2022 and 0.1 percentage point in 2023, reducing its global GDP growth estimates to 3.8% this year and 3.4% in 2023. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1135 ET - The conflict in Ukraine is expected to bring sanctions that may limit the amount of grain exports out of both Ukraine and Russia -- but that isn't likely to filter all the way down to US consumers, says US agriculture secretary Tom Vilsack. "We in the US are fortunate, we have tremendous production capacity," Vilsack says. He adds that US domestic farming is likely to mitigate any of the price increases that may occur for grains overseas, while European consumers are more at risk for higher prices. "I don't forsee a circumstance where US consumers will feel the uptick," Vilsack says. CBOT grain futures are higher in trading Thursday. (kirk.maltais@wsj.com; @kirkmaltais)\par \par \uc2 1130 ET - Treasury yields bounce back from early lows as Russia attacks Ukraine and global leaders threaten with sanctions. The 10-year now trades at 1.939%, up from an intraday low of 1.847%. But the conflict is expected to keep weighing on yields, as spooked investors seek safety in \uc2 US government\uc2  debt, potentially giving some pause to monetary authorities. "No one will be surprised if the Fed eases their approach if the situation hasn't improved by the time of their next Open Market Committee meeting on March 15-16," says asset manager \uc2 Louis Navellier\uc2  . "It would seem likely that before then the \uc2 ECB\uc2  will make easing maneuvers, paving the way for the Fed." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1119 ET - A measure of expected price swings for the euro versus the dollar in the options market, or implied volatility, rises after Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine. EUR/USD one-week implied volatility is last around 8.5%, the highest level since November 2020, according to Refinitiv. "The bearish skew of the options demand is also telling, as one-week risk reversals plumb lows last seen in April 2020," Monex Europe analysts say, noting that risk reversals show a skew in favor of options that bet on EUR/USD falling. Uncertainty over the Ukraine-Russia conflict means "risks are tilted to the downside" for the euro, particularly given Europe's reliance on gas supplies from Russia, they say. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1103 ET - Cryptocurrencies could become a safe haven as authorities involved in the Russia-Ukraine conflict tighten cash withdraw rules, says \uc2 Nigel Green\uc2  , CEO of consulting firm deVere Group. Bitcoin has lost 9% since yesterday's intraday high and 22% from this month's intraday high on February 10. But Green says it could be bound to strengthen. "I think we might see [crypto] revert to being regarded as a safe haven asset as the situation in Ukraine develops because it is unconfiscatable - which could become extremely important as centralised authorities take drastic steps." Bitcoin is down 4% today. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1048 ET - Investors' reaction to the invasion of Ukraine shows that "markets had not fully priced in the likelihood of deeper conflict," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. "Against a backdrop of heightened concerns about Ukraine, inflation, and interest rates, it is important to remember that global economic growth remains above trend and Covid-19-related restrictions are being lifted." \uc2 UBS\uc2  says value and cyclical sectors stand to gain, "including energy, financials" and even the Eurozone. "Despite their proximity to the crisis in Ukraine...the forces of economic recovery remain powerful and are potentially still a more important driver of global markets than events in Eastern Europe." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2022 13:30 ET (18:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220224ei2o003ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
