\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Stocks Tumble, Safe-Haven Assets Soar As Russia Attacks Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 3,415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par 11:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Global stock markets tumble as Russia's attack on Ukraine's military infrastructure catches markets off guard. Government bonds, the U.S. dollar and the Swiss franc and gold rise as investors pile into safe-haven assets. Oil prices jump and the Russian ruble plummets to record lows, prompting a pledge of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  . The following is a selection of analysts' comments.\par \par \uc2 Euro Could Fall Further Vs Dollar as Russia Invades Ukraine\par \par \uc2 1035 GMT - The euro could fall further against the dollar, even if investors scale back expectations for U.S. interest rate rises, as the eurozone's economy is vulnerable to the repercussions of Russia's invasion of Ukraine, ING says. "While the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  tightening cycle may be re-priced lower, we would still favor the dollar to outperform Europe right now," ING analysts say. Trade and energy links between the U.S. and Russia are "tiny" compared to Russia and the rest of Europe and dollar liquidity will be in demand during very uncertain periods like this, they say. EUR/USD could fall towards 1.1120, they say. EUR/USD drops 1.1% to a three-and-a-half-week low of 1.1175, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Pound Likely to Extend Losses Vs Dollar on Russia-Ukraine Crisis\par \par \uc2 1028 GMT - Sterling should weaken further versus the dollar after Russia launched an unprecedented invasion of Ukraine, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. The market had already lowered its U.K. interest rate rise bets in recent days after comments from \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  officials and could further rein in those expectations in response to the conflict, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a note. "The scaling back of more aggressive BOE rate hike expectations in the near-term combined with more risk-off trading conditions should see cable [GBP/USD] correct lower and test year to date lows at 1.3358 in the coming weeks," he says, noting the dollar's safe haven status. GBP/USD falls 0.9% to a three-and-a-half-week low of 1.3419, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 UK-Listed Airline Stocks Fall After Russia Attacks Ukraine\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT - London-listed airline stocks fall after Russia attacked Ukraine and the U.S. said it was preparing sanctions. \uc2 Wizz Air\uc2  said it was temporarily suspending flights to the area after the country closed its air space. The airline operates around 117 routes from six airports in the country. Shares in \uc2 Wizz Air\uc2  were down 11% at 3,255 pence, with \uc2 IAG\uc2  --owner of \uc2 British Airways\uc2  and Vueling, among others--down 6.6% at 146.47 pence and \uc2 easyJet\uc2  down 6.6% at 603.60 pence. Shares in German travel operator \uc2 TUI\uc2  were down 5.1% at 235.70 pence, while \uc2 Jet2\uc2  was down 2.5% at 1,254 pence. (anthony.orunagoriainoff@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Luxury's High-End Players Most at Direct Risk From Russia-Ukraine Crisis\par \par \uc2 1019 GMT - Companies operating at the higher end of luxury goods are most directly vulnerable to conflict between Russia and Ukraine, Bernstein says. The two countries make up around 4%-5% of total demand, and given their extreme income and wealth inequality, sales there are skewed toward the more expensive end of the luxury spectrum, Bernstein says. High-end operators like Cartier owner Richemont are as such most vulnerable to the direct effects of conflict, while those that appeal to the aspirational bourgeoisie are less exposed, Bernstein says, pointing to French groups \uc2 LVMH\uc2  and Kering as examples. (joshua.kirby@wsj.com; @joshualeokirby)\par \par \uc2 European Stocks Tumble as Russia-Ukraine Conflict Drags\par \par \uc2 1012 GMT - European stocks lose ground as Russia's military assault on Ukraine spooks investors. The Stoxx Europe 600, DAX and CAC 40 fall more than 3% and the FTSE 100 is down 2.7%. Brent crude surges 7% to $100.77 a barrel and gold and silver prices rise 2% and 3%, respectively as traders flock to safe havens. "Shares continued to tumble globally Thursday, with all European sectors down as investors fled riskier assets amid worsening military conflict in Ukraine," ActivTrades says. "It's now harder and harder for investors to predict where markets are going to go next as many expect the U.S. and its allies to proceed with strong retaliation measures in the shape of deeper economic sanctions." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Safe-Haven Assets to Remain in Demand Until There Is Clarity\par \par \uc2 1011 GMT - Safe-haven assets such as German Bunds, the U.S. dollar and gold are likely to remain in demand until there's more clarity regarding the escalating situation in Ukraine, Donner & Reuschel says. "The decisive factors now are how long the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  will last, how far Russian troops will advance into Ukraine and what the reactions will be from the West and China," Carsten Mumm, chief economist of the bank, says. The situation is still too unclear to take a new position, he says, adding that in the past, armed conflicts have usually only resulted in short-term setbacks on stock markets. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 US Dollar Seen as Attractive Tactical \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Amid Ukraine Conflict\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT - Investors looking to boost their portfolios should brace for strength in the U.S. dollar, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says, after Russian missiles and airstrikes hit Ukraine's capital, Kyiv, and more than a dozen other cities across the country. The Swiss bank notes the U.S. dollar is a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that tends to rally during periods of heightened geopolitical uncertainty. "We think that market expectations of six or seven U.S. interest rate hikes are likely to support the US dollar in the months ahead. As such, we see the U.S. dollar as an attractive tactical \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position at present," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. (mauro.orru@wsj.com; @MauroOrru94)\par \par \uc2 Russia-Ukraine Escalation Seen Supporting Case for More Protective Assets\par \par \uc2 0948 GMT - The possibility that the escalation of the Russia-Ukraine conflict sends inflation to new heights adds to the case for additional protection and a prudent approach to risk assets, \uc2 Amundi\uc2  says. The asset manager prefers European equities with a value tilt and is cautious on the highest growth areas of the market. \uc2 Amundi\uc2  has reduced its exposure to credit risk, in particular, to euro-denominated investment-grade bonds, and added hedges, it says. "The idea that the local crisis could morph into a global situation suggests that we should add protection regarding U.S. equity markets as well as U.S. credit high yield," \uc2 Amundi\uc2  says. (maitane.sardon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Investors Should Reconsider Credit Risk\par \par \uc2 0941 GMT - Investors should reconsider credit risk, says \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  . While high-yield corporate bonds have outperformed investment-grade bonds since the beginning of the year, there are signs that credit risk might start to crumble, the Copenhagen-based bank says, adding that no junk bond has been issued since Feb. 10 on heightened market volatility. Also, leverage loan indexes are falling on both sides of the Atlantic and real rates rising, it says. Risk assets come under renewed pressure Thursday as Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, with the pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 down 2.8%. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 European Banks' Shares Are Buffetted After Russia Attacks Ukraine\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT - European bank stocks trade sharply lower after Russia attacks Ukraine, prompting Western countries to prepare broad sanctions against Moscow. \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank International\uc2  leads the decline with a 14% drop. The Vienna-listed bank made 33% of its pretax profit in Russia last year and is by far the most exposed out of large listed European banks. Its shares have lost 30% of their value in the past week amid escalating conflict in the region. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  and \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  , which made about 6% and 4% of their pretax profit in Russia according to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  analysts, fall 6.8% and 7.1%, respectively. Major European banks also trade deep in the red, with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  falling 6%, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  6.4% lower and \uc2 UBS\uc2  down 5.8%. (cristina.roca@wsj.com; @_cristinaroca)\par \par \uc2 Russian Ruble Seen Facing Further Sharp Falls\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - The Russian ruble faces further significant losses even as \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilize market conditions, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday said it would start \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the ruble hit a record low after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched an invasion of Ukraine. Market participants already expected the bank would need to raise interest rates aggressively before the conflict, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a note. "It is likely that ruble volatility will remain at more extreme levels with the ruble at risk of further sharp sell offs in the days and weeks ahead." USD/RUB rises 5.5% to 85.1960 after hitting a record high of 89.9900 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Markets Hadn't Priced in Likelihood of Deeper Ukraine Conflict\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT - Heightened volatility as the conflict in Ukraine escalates is a sign that markets hadn't fully priced in the likelihood of deeper conflict with Russia, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. "We expect continued volatility in the near term as leaders calibrate and announce their response to this escalation," the Swiss bank says, after Russian missiles and air strikes hit Ukraine's capital, Kyiv, and more than a dozen other cities across the country. Energy prices could go higher, while oil-reliant economies could see economic activity take a hit in non-energy sectors, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. (mauro.orru@wsj.com; @MauroOrru94)\par \par \uc2 European Business and Consumer Confidence Face Hit, Inflation to Rise on Ukraine Conflict\par \par \uc2 24 Feb 2022 05:58 ET  Market Talk Roundup: Stocks Tumble, Safe-Haven -2-\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT - The European economy could suffer a significant setback from Russia's invasion of Ukraine, Berenberg says. Negative shocks to business and consumer confidence as well as even higher energy prices and thus inflation would dampen growth near-term, economists at Berenberg say. The rebound in eurozone economic growth following Omicron's setback could be delayed by up to two months, but a eurozone recession--two consecutive quarterly GDP contractions-- seems unlikely to Berenberg. In the medium term, economic growth in the eurozone should be back on track for a strong post-Omicron rebound amid easing supply shortages, the German bank says. But inflation could stay slightly more elevated, around 0.3 percentage points up, due to somewhat higher energy prices, Berenberg says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Swedish Krona Vulnerable if Full-Scale War Breaks Out in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT - The Swedish krona is vulnerable if a full-scale war breaks out in Ukraine, and SEB says it can't rule out EUR/SEK rising above 11.00. Rapidly rising energy prices provide additional fuel for inflation, while equity market falls have a negative effect on growth and confidence barometers, SEB says. "The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  's reaction to what is happening should, if anything, become dovish, which in [the] short term will cement the krona's handicap as a low-interest rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as sensitive to geopolitical risk." EUR/SEK trades 0.9% higher on the day at 10.7148, having earlier hit its highest since May 2020 at 10.7440, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 Drops as Ukraine Conflict Hits Stocks\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT - The FTSE 100 Index slumps 2.7%, or 206 points, to 7292 as the outbreak of hostilities in Ukraine broadly weighs on stocks. Russia-focused miner \uc2 Polymetal International\uc2  falls 43%, Russian steel maker Evraz is down 30% and a range of financial and travel-related stocks also fall after Moscow began attacking its eastern European neighbor. Shares in \uc2 WPP\uc2  drop 6% even as the advertising, marketing and public-relations firm reported a swing to pretax profit for 2021. \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2  retreats 8% after the U.K. bank's fourth-quarter profit missed expectations. Still, \uc2 BAE Systems\uc2  rises 3% after the arms group said pretax profit rose in 2021 on higher sales. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 EUR/CHF Risks Fall Toward 1.0 as Russian Money Could Head to Switzerland\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT - EUR/CHF hit a record low of 1.0292 in early trade Thursday, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  , as Russia's attack on Ukraine prompts investors to scramble for safe-haven assets, and ING analysts say the possibility a drop towards 1.0 can't be ruled out. "Investors could speculate that Russian money will be heading to Switzerland and out of dollars, ahead of likely more aggressive sanctions," they say in a research note. "Until [the] Ukraine tension is resolved, expect EUR/CHF to stay offered and we cannot rule out a test of 1.00 if the situation deteriorates further." (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Ukraine War Expected to Soften BOE, \uc2 ECB\uc2  Tone at March Meetings\par \par \uc2 0849 GMT - Rate-setters at the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to sound a more cautious tone at their respective upcoming meetings in March as war grips Europe, says Berenberg. Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine is likely to soften the communication surrounding these central banks' decisions. Both central banks are likely to "tread more cautiously at their March meetings" providing "even stronger emphasis on keeping options open upon charting the course of stimulus removal," it says. Yields on \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  debt fall as investors seek haven assets. Yields move inversely to bond prices. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Drop Due to Geographical Proximity to Ukraine\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  most vulnerable after Russia's attack on Ukraine, aside from the ruble itself, are those geographically closest to the crisis and exposed to the imported energy story, ING analysts say in a note. The Czech koruna, Hungarian forint and Polish zloty drop sharply, while the Swedish krona also "looks exposed," ING says. The Swedish krona earlier fell to its lowest against the euro since May 2020, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . EUR/CZK is last up 1.4% at 24.9198, while EUR/PLN rises 0.9% and EUR/HUF is up by 0.6%. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 UK Gilts Gain as Investors Turn to Safe-Haven Assets\par \par \uc2 0834 GMT - Investors shed risk assets and rotate into perceived safe-haven securities, including \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  debt, as Russia begins a full-scale invasion of Ukraine. Gilts gain as the yield on the 10-year U.K. benchmark government bond, which moves inversely to the price of the bond, falls four basis points to 1.426%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . The move follows a similar flight to safety in other government bonds, including U.S. Treasurys and German sovereign debt. "It's panic in the markets," says Ipek Ozkardeskaya, senior analyst at \uc2 Swissquote\uc2  (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Dollar, Safe-Haven \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rise as Russia Attacks Ukraine\par \par \uc2 0826 GMT - The dollar, Swiss franc and yen rise as investors seek safe-haven assets after news that Russia has launched a military attack on Ukraine, with missiles and air strikes hitting the capital. "Dollar liquidity will be in demand at very uncertain times like this," ING analysts say in a note, adding that they expect the DXY dollar index to rise to 97.00. The DXY is last up 0.4% at 96.6130, having earlier hit a three-and-a-half week high of 96.7650. The Swiss franc rose to a record high against the euro overnight, with EUR/CHF falling to 1.0292, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  , while a drop in the Russian ruble to record lows prompted the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to say it would start \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Ukraine Conflict Could Add Up to 1.5% to Eurozone's Inflation This Year\par \par \uc2 0823 GMT - Russia's attack on Ukraine is likely to cause a sharp rise in energy prices, increasing average headline inflation in the eurozone by 1.5% this year, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. The economic-research firm forecasts that oil prices could settle between $120 and $140 a barrel, while gas prices could return to the all-time high of EUR180 a megawatt hour. However, the impact of higher prices could be mitigated by fiscal measures or alternative solutions to increase supply, CE says. "Eventually, oil trade flows would be re-routed which should bring prices back down. It might take longer for gas prices to fall, but we wouldn't expect them to stay at EUR180 indefinitely unless there were an irrevocable breakdown in EU-Russian economic relations," it says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Gold, Safe Havens Rally as Russian Invasion Hits Risk Appetite\par \par \uc2 0813 GMT - Gold prices rise as investors flock to safe havens after Russia's invasion of Ukraine. Comex gold futures rose 1.8% to $1,944 a troy ounce, their highest level in over a year. Global stock markets dropped and gold, the dollar, and bonds--assets considered safe havens in times of turmoil--all rose. "The Russian invasion of Ukraine puts the markets in panic mode," German refiner \uc2 Heraeus Precious Metals\uc2  says. "Investors are throwing shares out of their portfolios and fleeing to safe havens." (william.horner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Flight to Safety Dominates Bond Markets\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT - Flight to safety is dominating markets as Russia attacks Ukraine, with eurozone government bond yields falling sharply across the board, led by the safe-haven German Bund. "Market expectations regarding [Russian President Vladimir] Putin's next steps will determine the way forward," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategists say. "Given the high uncertainty in that regard as well as regarding the \uc2 NATO\uc2  response, fears of Russian troops crossing further Rubicons should loom large." \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategists expect flight to safety to intensify as markets are facing the prospect of a "very long, headline prone week-end," they add. The 10-year German Bund yield is trading 8.6 basis points lower at 0.139%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty, Risk-Off Dominate Markets\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT - Uncertainty and risk-off is the name of the game for Thursday, \uc2 KBC Bank\uc2  's analysts say, after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered a special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to target military facilities across Ukraine. \uc2 KBC\uc2  's analysts say that while the market impact could eventually fade, they recommend investors to err on the side of caution. "Although the market impact of geopolitical events tends to fade after a short period of time, we'd advise not to row against the current tide," they say. Eurozone government bond yields--which move inversely to prices--trade sharply lower in a sign of investors' flight-to-safety. The 10-year German Bund yield trades 7 basis points lower at 0.155%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Eurozone Government Bond Yields Drop as Russia Targets Ukraine's Military Infrastructure\par \par \uc2 0717 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields slide in early trade in risk-off mode after Russia conducts attacks on Ukrainian military infrastructure. The 10-year German Bund yield trades 7 basis points lower at 0.155% and 10-year bond yields of peer eurozone countries also drop, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . The 10-year French OAT yield trades 5 bps lower at 0.681%, and the 10-year Italian BTP yield is down 2.5 bps at 1.907%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Oil Surges as Russian Invasion Begins\par \par \uc2 24 Feb 2022 05:58 ET  Market Talk Roundup: Stocks Tumble, Safe-Haven -3-\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT - Oil prices surge but fall short of hitting the keenly watched $100 a barrel level after Russia launches an invasion of Ukraine. Brent crude oil continuous futures jump 5% to $98.66 a barrel, their highest level since 2014. Front-month Brent futures which expire in April jumped as high as $102.07 a barrel. U.S. benchmark WTI futures rose over 5% to $96.89 a barrel. Russian missiles and airstrikes hit dozens of cities throughout Ukraine early on Thursday, immediately after President Putin said he would launch an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  against Ukraine. Investors fear Russia's plentiful oil supplies could get snarled in a conflict or in the ensuing sanctions that are expected to target the Kremlin at a time when energy markets are already tight. (william.horner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2022 05:58 ET (10:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220224ei2o001jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
