\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par 10:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0951 GMT - Malaysia's inflation risks seem to remain tilted to the upside despite the consumer-price index moderating for the second straight month to 2.3% in January, \uc2 UOB\uc2  says. Among key upside risks include global supply-chain disruptions, escalating commodity prices, higher wages due to post-pandemic labor shortages and a revised national minimum wage, \uc2 UOB\uc2  says. The moderation in CPI for January was purely a result of high base effects, partially cushioned by higher prices in most goods and services, particularly food and beverages, the bank adds. \uc2 UOB\uc2  maintains its 2022 full-year inflation forecast at 3.0%. (chester.tay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0948 GMT - The possibility that the escalation of the Russia-Ukraine conflict sends inflation to new heights adds to the case for additional protection and a prudent approach to risk assets, \uc2 Amundi\uc2  says. The asset manager prefers European equities with a value tilt and is cautious on the highest growth areas of the market. \uc2 Amundi\uc2  has reduced its exposure to credit risk, in particular, to euro-denominated investment-grade bonds, and added hedges, it says. "The idea that the local crisis could morph into a global situation suggests that we should add protection regarding U.S. equity markets as well as U.S. credit high yield," \uc2 Amundi\uc2  says. (maitane.sardon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0941 GMT - Investors should reconsider credit risk, says \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  . While high-yield corporate bonds have outperformed investment-grade bonds since the beginning of the year, there are signs that credit risk might start to crumble, the Copenhagen-based bank says, adding that no junk bond has been issued since Feb. 10 on heightened market volatility. Also, leverage loan indexes are falling on both sides of the Atlantic and real rates rising, it says. Risk assets come under renewed pressure Thursday as Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, with the pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 down 2.8%. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT - European bank stocks trade sharply lower after Russia attacks Ukraine, prompting Western countries to prepare broad sanctions against Moscow. \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank International\uc2  leads the decline with a 14% drop. The Vienna-listed bank made 33% of its pretax profit in Russia last year and is by far the most exposed out of large listed European banks. Its shares have lost 30% of their value in the past week amid escalating conflict in the region. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  and \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  , which made about 6% and 4% of their pretax profit in Russia according to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  analysts, fall 6.8% and 7.1%, respectively. Major European banks also trade deep in the red, with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  falling 6%, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  6.4% lower and \uc2 UBS\uc2  down 5.8%. (cristina.roca@wsj.com; @_cristinaroca)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - The Russian ruble faces further significant losses even as \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilize market conditions, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday said it would start \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the ruble hit a record low after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched an invasion of Ukraine. Market participants already expected the bank would need to raise interest rates aggressively before the conflict, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a note. "It is likely that ruble volatility will remain at more extreme levels with the ruble at risk of further sharp sell offs in the days and weeks ahead." USD/RUB rises 5.5% to 85.1960 after hitting a record high of 89.9900 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT - The European economy could suffer a significant setback from Russia's invasion of Ukraine, Berenberg says. Negative shocks to business and consumer confidence as well as even higher energy prices and thus inflation would dampen growth near-term, economists at Berenberg say. The rebound in eurozone economic growth following Omicron's setback could be delayed by up to two months, but a eurozone recession--two consecutive quarterly GDP contractions-- seems unlikely to Berenberg. In the medium term, economic growth in the eurozone should be back on track for a strong post-Omicron rebound amid easing supply shortages, the German bank says. But inflation could stay slightly more elevated, around 0.3 percentage points up, due to somewhat higher energy prices, Berenberg says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT Currently trading at CAD 1.2810, the U.S. dollar is on the upside and stands above its 50-period moving average on a 30-min chart at CAD 1.2732. Moreover, the intraday RSI remains within its buying area and confirms the bullish bias. As a consequence, above horizontal support at CAD 1.2745, look for further advance toward horizontal resistance at CAD 1.2815 and toward Dec. 29 top at CAD 1.2835 in extension. A third target is set at 1.2855. Only a break below horizontal support at CAD 1.2745 would call for a weakness toward horizontal support at CAD 1.2725 and toward 1.2705 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT - The Swedish krona is vulnerable if a full-scale war breaks out in Ukraine, and SEB says it can't rule out EUR/SEK rising above 11.00. Rapidly rising energy prices provide additional fuel for inflation, while equity market falls have a negative effect on growth and confidence barometers, SEB says. "The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  's reaction to what is happening should, if anything, become dovish, which in [the] short term will cement the krona's handicap as a low-interest rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as sensitive to geopolitical risk." EUR/SEK trades 0.9% higher on the day at 10.7148, having earlier hit its highest since May 2020 at 10.7440, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT - EUR/CHF hit a record low of 1.0292 in early trade Thursday, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  , as Russia's attack on Ukraine prompts investors to scramble for safe-haven assets, and ING analysts say the possibility a drop towards 1.0 can't be ruled out. "Investors could speculate that Russian money will be heading to Switzerland and out of dollars, ahead of likely more aggressive sanctions," they say in a research note. "Until [the] Ukraine tension is resolved, expect EUR/CHF to stay offered and we cannot rule out a test of 1.00 if the situation deteriorates further." (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT - Taken at face value, the main takeaways from Sweden's Labor Force Survey is that employment has recovered well, says \uc2 Nordea\uc2  . The number of employed increased by 3.4% year-on-year in January while the employment rate reached new highs, it says. Unemployment stood at 8.1%, still much higher than the registered-based Swedish Public Employment Services unemployment at 7.3% in January, due to a large inflow of workers into the labor force, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  says. "Indicators point to a remarkable strong labor market ahead with new vacancies at all-time highs, incredibly high employment plans and labor shortages at record levels." (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0849 GMT - Rate-setters at the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to sound a more cautious tone at their respective upcoming meetings in March as war grips Europe, says Berenberg. Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine is likely to soften the communication surrounding these central banks' decisions. Both central banks are likely to "tread more cautiously at their March meetings" providing "even stronger emphasis on keeping options open upon charting the course of stimulus removal," it says. Yields on \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  debt fall as investors seek haven assets. Yields move inversely to bond prices. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  most vulnerable after Russia's attack on Ukraine, aside from the ruble itself, are those geographically closest to the crisis and exposed to the imported energy story, ING analysts say in a note. The Czech koruna, Hungarian forint and Polish zloty drop sharply, while the Swedish krona also "looks exposed," ING says. The Swedish krona earlier fell to its lowest against the euro since May 2020, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . EUR/CZK is last up 1.4% at 24.9198, while EUR/PLN rises 0.9% and EUR/HUF is up by 0.6%. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2022 04:51 ET (09:51 GMT)