\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Volatility Rules The Day As Russia Attacks Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 2,246 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par 22:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Markets remain turbulent after Russia's invasion of Ukraine, with US stocks finishing higher after selling off earlier in the day. The following is a selection of market reaction and comments from analysts and asset managers.\par \par \uc2 Dollar Rally Weakens as Markets Digest Attack on Ukraine\par \par \uc2 16:07 ET - The Ukraine-driven dollar rally stabilizes, with the WSJ US Dollar index rising nearly 1% to 90.36, off its intraday high of 90.90. The greenback gains around 1% versus the euro and the pound and 0.5% versus the yen. Elliot Wave's Murray Gunn tells WSJ the geopolitical tension favors the USD. "Given that the vast majority of global debt is denominated in US dollars, periods of 'risk-off' like this generally result in a scramble for dollars." He adds the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also a potential save haven "given Japan's domestic macroeconomic bias." Gunn expects the yen to strengthen for the time being. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 US Stocks Erase Big Losses as Investors Weigh Russia Fallout\par \par \uc2 16:05 ET - US stocks rebound from sharp early losses that briefly pushed the Nasdaq Composite into bear market territory and the Dow Industrials into correction region as the US announces sanctions on Russia that are not as deep as some had feared. Oil trims early gains that lifted prices temporarily over $100 after Russia invaded Ukraine, while gold turns lower and Treasury yields recover much of their early declines. Information technology and communications services are the best performing sectors, while, consumer staples, energy and financials fall sharply. DJIA gains 92 points to 33223, the S&P 500 gains 1.5% to 4288 and the Nasdaq jumps 3.3% to 13473. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Sanctions Seen as Hurting Russians, Driving Negative Growth\par \par \uc2 15:52 ET - Sanctions announced by the US and its allies "will have a significant impact [on] Russia's overall economy, and average Russians will feel the cost," says the IIF, the global association of the financial industry. "These sanctions target Russia's domestic financial system, causing bank runs and forcing \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue hiking rates. As a result, we are likely to see negative growth in an economy that has already been hindered by increasing isolationism." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 Treasury Yields Bounce Back From Ukraine-Driven Losses\par \par \uc2 15:47 ET - The 10-year Treasury yield closes little changed, at 1.969%, after sliding in the morning on news that Russia attacked Ukraine, while the 30-year manages to rise 0.017 percentage point to 2.291%. Investors wonder how the conflict might alter US monetary policy. "With the Fed already being perceived as 'behind the curve,' we doubt these events will stay the Fed's hand next month," BMO says. But given the economic risks and volatility, the war "will at the very least, likely temper some of the most aggressive calls on the Fed," such as a 50 basis point rate increase, according to BMO. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 Chips, Defense and Uranium ETFs Could See Upswing From Russia Invasion\par \par \uc2 14:56 ET - The war in Ukraine could boost some ETFs, Emles' Louis Ricci tells WSJ. An example is semiconductor ETF SMH, which is "a good play if you think attention turns to Taiwan after Ukraine," Ricci says. ETFs holding defense contractors, such as ITA, XAR and Emles' FEDX tend to "benefit from escalated tensions or from general defensive positioning in a down market." Ricci also points to China-focused KWEB and Russia-focused RSX as "places to go bottom fishing." Uranium-mining ETF URNM "is an interesting play if you think Europe...will greenlight more nuclear energy on the back of the shortages we are seeing now."(paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 Russia-Ukraine Fighting Sends Livestock Lower\par \par \uc2 14:56 ET - Russia's attack on Ukraine sent \uc2 CME\uc2  livestock futures toppling today, going in the opposite direction of grains futures. Live cattle futures fell 1.7% today to $1.423 per pound, while lean hog futures dropped 2.3% to $1.05525 per pound. "Impact from restrictions on Russian protein purchases in the world market are likely to have no impact on global trade," says Steiner Consulting Group. "However, Russia and Ukraine are major contributors to global grain and oil trade and they are also major suppliers of fertilizers. High feed and energy costs are negative for US livestock producers and they will negatively impact their ability to bring more product to market." President Biden said Thursday that a new wave of sanctions will be imposed on Russia, but did not release details. (kirk.maltais@wsj.com; @kirkmaltais)\par \par \uc2 Natural Gas Prices End Session Lower\par \par \uc2 14:50 ET - Natural gas prices finish the session 1.2% lower at $4.568/mmBtu after President Biden indicated in a press conference that despite Russia's invasion of Ukraine there were no immediate plans to sanction Russia in a way that would prevent it from exporting natural gas or crude oil. Gas prices had rallied more than 5% higher earlier in the trading day on the potential for Europe seeing its gas supplies from Russia suddenly halted, which may have meant US producers would have to fill the void. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Oil Ends Below $100 After Historic Surge on Russia-Ukraine\par \par \uc2 14:45 ET - US benchmark oil prices finish 0.8% higher at $92.81 a barrel, a somewhat anticlimactic finish for a session in which prices surged by more than 9% at one point to $100.54, which marked the first $100-plus price since 2014. The initial spike in prices was sparked by Russia's pre-dawn invasion of Ukraine. But a Biden press conference late in the trading session in which he indicated oil export sanctions against Russia weren't part of any immediate plan caused prices to reverse sharply. Brent crude, which hit a seven-year intraday high of $105.79, ended its session below $100 also. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Europe's Gas Woes Highlight Struggle with Energy Transition\par \par \uc2 14:03 ET - Europe's natural gas market, still licking its wounds from earlier this winter when prices soared amid weak wind production and the phasing out of coal, is in crisis mode again as gas supplier Russia invades Ukraine. "European natural gas prices are up 56% on the day to over $45/MCF which is over ten times the price in the U.S. at around $4.49/MCF," says Infracap founder and CEO Jay Hatfield, who says consumers can't afford that rise so it'll have to be absorbed by governments. "In addition, it graphically demonstrates that Europe has failed in its efforts to rapidly transition away from all hydrocarbons instead of just focusing on coal as COP26 recommended." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Russian Ruble's Sell-Off Seen Fuelling Rate Rise Bets\par \par \uc2 12:20 GMT - The Russian ruble's depreciation in reaction to the Ukraine crisis is likely to fuel expectations that \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise interest rates aggressively in coming months, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "USD/RUB may be trading below its early morning highs just below the 90.00 area, but the RUB is the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world this year," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. USD/RUB rises 7.4% to 87.3706, having earlier reached a record high of 89.9900, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . The ruble has pared some of its earlier losses after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and market participants are likely betting on further rate rises to bolster the ruble, Foley says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 European Stocks Slide as Russia-Ukraine Conflict Weighs\par \par \uc2 12:20 ET - European stocks slump in closing trade as Russia's full-scale military invasion of Ukraine dents market sentiment. The Stoxx Europe 600 falls 3.3%, the FTSE 100 drops 3.9%, the DAX declines 3.9% and the CAC slips 3.8%. Anglo-Russian precious metals mining company \uc2 Polymetal International\uc2  is biggest faller on the Stoxx with its shares plunging 37.8%. European bank stocks also tumble. Energy stocks rise as oil prices jump. "The escalation of tensions arising from the Russian action has pulled the rug from markets, adding to an already brittle environment in the face of rising inflation and interest rate concerns," \uc2 Interactive Investor\uc2  says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Swedish Krona Is Likely to Remain Under Pressure\par \par \uc2 12:18 ET - The Swedish krona is the worst performing G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday due to Sweden's sensitivity to Russia's military invasion of Ukraine, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "Neither Sweden nor Finland are members of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and their geographical position around the Baltic sea ensures that whether or not they should be in \uc2 NATO\uc2  is a topical and sensitive discussion," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "The SEK could remain under pressure as long as Russia remains on the offensive." EUR/SEK rises 0.5% to 10.6737 after hitting a 21-month high of 10.7570 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Dollar Gets Extra Boost from Ukraine Conflict\par \par \uc2 12:01 ET - The Ukraine conflict adds to the tailwind already boosting the US dollar, says Nancy Lazar, from \uc2 Piper Sandler\uc2 . "Prior to the invasion, we were expecting a stronger dollar on the back of the [global] slowdown we've been expecting," she says, adding that now the "Russia/Ukraine war will put even more upward pressure on the dollar." The greenback strengthens against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the euro and the pound, and the WSJ US Dollar Index rises 1.1%. Longer term, the USD/EUR is up 9% in 12 months. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 Coke Bottler Halts Production in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 11:46 ET - Coke bottler \uc2 Coca-Cola HBC\uc2  is halting production in Ukraine after Russia launched a military attack on the country. The bottler also does business in Russia. "The safety of our people is our number one priority and we have enacted our contingency plans that include stopping production in the Ukraine, closing our plant and asking colleagues in the country to remain at home and follow local guidance," the bottling company said. "We will keep this under review over the coming days." (jennifer.maloney@wsj.com; @maloneyfiles)\par \par \uc2 Norwegian Cruise Line\uc2  Says Ukraine Conflict Could Disrupt Summer Sailings\par \par \uc2 24 Feb 2022 16:45 ET  Market Talk Roundup: Volatility Rules The Day As -2-\par \par \uc2 11:41 ET - The conflict between Russia and Ukraine could affect \uc2 Norwegian Cruise Line Holdings\uc2  ' scheduled trips through the Baltics this summer, Chief Executive \uc2 Frank Del Rio\uc2  says on the company's 4Q conference call. About 5% of the company's capacity, or about 50 sailings, call on the port of St. Petersburg over the course of the summer season, he says. The company is currently assessing what other ports in the Scandinavian region could replace St. Petersburg, which Del Rio characterizes as a premier destination. (will.feuer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Oil Stays Sharply Higher After Mixed EIA Data\par \par \uc2 11:36 ET - Oil markets are mostly shrugging off a weekly EIA report on US oil inventories that offered a mix of bullish and bearish data points, as they remain laser-focused on Russia-Ukraine developments. The report showed a second straight weekly increase in US crude inventories as they climbed by a hefty 4.5 million barrels. But gasoline and other fuel inventories fell week-on-week, and crude stockpiles at the main US hub in Cushing, Okla. dropped sharply to just 23.8M barrels, the lowest since 2018. US oil production remained unchanged for a second straight week at 11.6M bpd. WTI stays higher, up 5.3% at $96.97 a barrel. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Global GDP Growth Forecast Cut for 2022 and 2023 Due to Ukraine Conflict\par \par \uc2 11:36 ET - Due to the latest events in the Russia-Ukraine conflict, Oxford Economics expects higher European gas, oil and food prices over the medium term. The economic research firm also expects more financial market disruption and tougher \uc2 European Union\uc2  and U.S. sanctions on Russia. "Higher inflation is the main transmission channel for a weaker economic outlook," Oxford Economics says. The conflict and all its spillover effects leads Oxford Economics to cut its global GDP growth forecast by 0.2 percentage points in 2022 and 0.1 percentage point in 2023, reducing its global GDP growth estimates to 3.8% this year and 3.4% in 2023. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Ukraine Turmoil Won't Raise Consumer Food Prices, Vilsack Says\par \par \uc2 11:35 ET - The conflict in Ukraine is expected to bring sanctions that may limit the amount of grain exports out of both Ukraine and Russia -- but that isn't likely to filter all the way down to US consumers, says US agriculture secretary Tom Vilsack. "We in the US are fortunate, we have tremendous production capacity," Vilsack says. He adds that US domestic farming is likely to mitigate any of the price increases that may occur for grains overseas, while European consumers are more at risk for higher prices. "I don't forsee a circumstance where US consumers will feel the uptick," Vilsack says. CBOT grain futures are higher in trading Thursday. (kirk.maltais@wsj.com; @kirkmaltais)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2022 16:45 ET (21:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220224ei2o004aj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
