\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 More than 100 killed, 300 injured, 10,000 displaced in first 24 hours of terror in Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 1,124 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Ukraine decreed this Friday a general mobilization to try to stop an all-out Russian offensive that in less than 24 hours approached Kiev, leaving more than 100 dead and some 100,000 displaced.The invasion provoked in response a reinforcement of Western economic sanctions against Russia, judged insufficient by the Ukrainian president, Volodomir Zelenski, who regretted that his country has been left "alone" to face the Russian troops. The general mobilization will affect people subject to "compulsory military service and reservists" and will be in force for 90 days, said the decree issued by Zelenski.At least 137 people were killed, both civilians and soldiers, and 316 were wounded as a result of the air and ground attacks, said the president.\par \par \uc2 Read also: \uc2 European Union\uc2  leaders agree on sanctions package against RussiaRussian troops entered the north, south and east of the country and seized a strategic air base near Kiev, as well as the area of the Chernobyl power plant, still contaminated by radioactivity from the 1986 nuclear accident, when Ukraine was part of the now dismembered Soviet Union. The Gostomel air base fell after an attack carried out by soldiers transported by helicopter from Belarus, a country allied with Russia, witnesses reported."The helicopters arrived and the fighting started. They were firing with machine guns and grenade launchers," said one of the witnesses, Serguiy Storojouk.That airfield could serve as an outpost to launch an offensive on Kiev where, according to President Zelenski, Russian "sabotage groups" have already entered. Early Friday morning, the capital woke up again to loud explosions, AFP reported. And in the Sumy region (northeast), bordering Russia and not far from Kiev, all municipalities "are surrounded" and "many" Russian armored vehicles marched towards Kiev, its governor Serhiy Zhyvytskiy told the UNIAN agency.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainians flee the war About 100,000 people fled their homes in Ukraine and thousands sought refuge abroad following the Russian invasion, the \uc2 UN\uc2  said Thursday. "We cannot yet confirm exact figures, but it is clear that there was significant displacement inside the country and some movement across borders," a spokeswoman for the \uc2 UN High Commissioner for Refugees\uc2  (\uc2 UNHCR\uc2 ), Shabia Mantoo, told AFP."We think that about 100,000 people have already fled their homes and may have moved inside the country and that several thousand crossed international borders," she said. \uc2 Filippo Grandi\uc2 , \uc2 UN High Commissioner for Refugees\uc2 , announced during the day the reinforcement of \uc2 UNHCR\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capacity in Ukraine and neighboring countries.He also called on the governments of those countries to "leave borders open for people seeking safety and protection" coming from Ukraine."We stand ready to support everyone's efforts to respond to any situation of forced displacement," he wrote in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Russia welcomes "success" The \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  said all missions on the first day of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  "were successfully completed." President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  announced the offensive during the early hours of Thursday morning. The Russian army claimed that it destroyed 74 military installations, including 11 airfields, and that separatists in eastern Ukraine are advancing and taking control of territories.For its part, the Ukrainian armed forces estimated the damage inflicted on the Russian army at more than 30 tanks, up to 130 combat vehicles, 7 airplanes and 6 helicopters. Putin, who demands that \uc2 NATO\uc2  closes its doors to Ukraine, assured that he does not seek the "occupation" of this former Soviet republic, but "a demilitarization and denazification" of the country and to defend the pro-Russian rebels, whose independence he recognized on Monday.Nearly 100,000 people fled their homes and thousands sought refuge abroad, according to the \uc2 UN\uc2 . Olena Kurilo was injured by shattered glass in her home caused by shelling in Chuguev, near Kharkov."I will never, under any conditions, surrender to Putin. Better to die," said the 52-year-old teacher, her face covered with bandages.\par \par \uc2 Sanctions U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced restrictions on exports to and technology imports from Russia, as well as sanctions against Russian banks and tycoons. Japan and Canada decreed similar measures against Moscow.The \uc2 European Union\uc2  (EU) also decided to adopt sanctions with "massive and severe" consequences against Russia that will focus on the financial sector, energy, transport, export control and visa restrictions.The sanctions package "will increase inflation, accelerate capital outflows and progressively affect the industrial base" of Russia, said the President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 . French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , who spoke to Putin during the day to "call for an immediate end" to the offensive, stressed the importance of sanctions, but also of "leaving the way open" for an eventual dialogue with the Russian leader, when "the conditions are met". The invasion has hit the international markets. Brent crude oil topped $100 per barrel for the first time in seven years and European stock markets plummeted on Thursday, although Wall Street closed in the green and Asian bourses followed suit at their open on Friday. The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble, hit an all-time low against the dollar before \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The fall of Chernobyl The Ukrainian government announced the seizure by Russian troops of the Chernobyl nuclear power plant."After a fierce battle we lost control of the Chernobyl site," said Mikhail Podoliak, chief advisor to the office of President Volodomir Zelensky. After the loss of control of this area still heavily contaminated by the worst nuclear accident in history, the state of the plant's facilities, of the casing that isolates the accident reactor and of a nuclear fuel storage tank is unknown, said the senior official.\par \par \uc2 You may be interested in: "Mom, Dad, I love you": the heartbreaking video of a Ukrainian soldier before leaving for combat"This is one of the biggest threats to Europe today," he said, estimating that the Russians could organize "provocative" activities at the site to blame Ukraine. The \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  (\uc2 IAEA\uc2 ) called for "maximum restraint to avoid any action that would endanger the country's nuclear sites."\par \par \uc2 Protesters detained in Russia Russian police detained more than 1,700 people in several cities for participating in demonstrations against the war in Ukraine, according to human rights NGO OVD-info. On the streets of Moscow, there were residents expressing their concern and others their support for Putin. "I'm not happy, I'm very nervous," said Nikita Grushin, a 34-year-old businessman, saying he had no idea who "is right" in this crisis."I'm not going to criticize an order of the supreme commander. If he thinks this is necessary, it must be done," said Ivan, a 32-year-old engineer.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220225ei2o0031q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
