\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 World financial markets fall as Russia and Ukraine crisis escalates\b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 World stock markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and safe-haven assets fell after Russia launched a military offensive against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Thus, the price of a barrel of oil surpassed 100 dollars on Thursday, up 8.78%, for the first time in more than seven years. Brent crude advanced 2.6% to 99.36 dollars a barrel; while in New York, West Texas Intermediate (WTI) for delivery in April increased 8.66% to 100.10 dollars.\par \par \uc2 European stock exchanges fell sharply from the first exchanges. By midday, the Frankfurt stock exchange had lost more than 5%, followed by Milan and Paris (almost -4%), and Madrid and London, with losses of around 3%.\par \par \uc2 Moscow's capital park fell by more than 25% and the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble, reached its historic low against the dollar, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The negative trend also took hold in Asia. Hong Kong lost 3.21%, Tokyo closed with a drop of 1.81% and Shanghai fell by 1.70%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  opened Thursday with sharp falls, as investors moved away from the stock markets seeking safer havens after the start of the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 In early trading, the Dow Jones was down 2.30%; the tech-heavy Nasdaq index was down 2.74%, and the broader S&P 500 index was down 2.32%.\par \par \uc2 Safe-haven assets rise in valueThe threat of war sparked fears about the supply of commodities such as wheat and metals.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, grain prices broke a record for trading in Europe and wheat rose to a high of 344 euros per ton on the Euronext platform.\par \par \uc2 Wheat and corn prices, of which Ukraine is the world's fourth largest producer, rose sharply at the opening, a few hours after Russia launched an invasion against Kiev.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, assets considered safe havens, such as gold rose by 2.31% to 1,953 dollars an ounce; the dollar and the Japanese yen, also appreciated.\par \par \uc2 "Russian-Ukrainian tensions lead to a potential demand shock [in Europe] and a larger supply shock for the rest of the world, given the importance of Russia and Ukraine in energy," noted Tamas Strickland of \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For a \uc2 Swissquote\uc2  analyst, "rising energy prices are a big headache for Europe, because 40% of its natural gas and 30% of its oil come from Russia."\par \par \uc2 With regard to natural gas, the reference market in Europe advanced 21% more than the day before.\par \par \uc2 With information from AFP\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220224ei2o0080j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
