\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Russian stock market plummets almost 30% and the price of a barrel of oil rises above US$ 100 for the first time in seven years\b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Russian Moex stock index returned to trading after its suspension with a 40% plunge, now shrinking its sharp fall and losing almost 30%. For its part, the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble, reached its historic low against the dollar -before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -, after the launching of a military offensive by Russia against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Before the opening, the Moscow Stock Exchange announced in a brief statement that it was suspending "trading in all its markets until further notice".\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the price of a barrel of oil exceeds US$ 100 for the first time in more than seven years and world stock markets fall after the launching of a military offensive by Russia against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The European stock exchanges have fallen sharply since the first exchanges. Thus, the Frankfurt stock exchange lost 3.73%, followed by Paris (3.15%), Milan (3.10%), Madrid around 3% and London (2.45%).\par \par \uc2 In Asia, Hong Kong lost 3.24%. Tokyo closed with a fall of 1.81% and Shanghai, with a decline of 1.70%.\par \par \uc2 Russian President, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , announced early Thursday morning the start of a "military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Ukraine. Kiev claims it is a "far-reaching invasion".\par \par \uc2 "At the moment it is impossible to bet on any scenario," points out Ipek Ozkardeskaya, an analyst at investment company \uc2 SwissQuote\uc2 , for whom "it's panic on the markets."\par \par \uc2 "We can only follow the latest developments closely and be prepared for more volatility," he added.\par \par \uc2 The threat of war sparked fears over the supply of commodities such as wheat and metals in recent weeks as economies reopened after shutdowns due to the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Other assets considered safe havens, such as gold, the dollar and the Japanese yen, also rose in value.\par \par \uc2 World authorities have tried unsuccessfully to contain the conflict and Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski said he tried to communicate with Putin but was not answered.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  deplored Russia's "unwarranted attack" and warned that it "will bring catastrophic loss of life and human suffering."\par \par \uc2 "Russian-Ukrainian tensions lead to a potential demand shock [in Europe] and a larger supply shock for the rest of the world, given the importance of Russia and Ukraine in energy," noted Tamas Strickland of \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Regarding natural gas, the reference market in Europe, the Dutch TTF, was trading this Thursday at 107.615 euros (about 121 dollars) per megawatt hour (MWh), 21% more than the day before.\par \par \uc2 The crisis comes at a time when governments around the world are struggling to contain inflation, fueled by rising demand due to the global economic reopening.\par \par \uc2 Much of the attention is focused on the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  (Fed), whose authorities are preparing to raise interest rates in March to contain rising prices.\par \par \uc2 SN\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220224ei2o0056q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
