\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Reaction in the markets after Russia's offensive: commodities rise and stock markets fall by up to 4%.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,318 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  announced a military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine to fend off separatists in the east of the country. "I have made a decision on a military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Putin declared in an unexpected TV message shortly before 03H00 GMT. In the message he called on the Ukrainian military to "lay down their arms." As expected, markets react in the worst way. Oil skyrocketed to u$s100, gas became 30% more expensive. Stock markets fall up to 3%.\par \par \uc2 Oil\par \par \uc2 The price of a barrel of oil surpassed 100 dollars on Thursday for the first time in more than seven years. Brent North Sea crude oil in its May contract settled at u$s101.84 with a jump of almost 9%, while West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude oil traded in the New York futures market in its April contract surpassed u$s100 a barrel with a rise of 8%. Brent crude oil after the escalation of the conflict reached u$s103, its highest level since August 2014.\par \par \uc2 Since the tension between the two countries began, the cost of a barrel has increased by 25%. It should be taken into account that Russia is the second largest oil exporter in the world and this may threaten the global recovery.\par \par \uc2 The level of harshness of the new economic sanctions that are being prepared to stifle the Kremlin economically will be key to gauge how far the rise in energy commodity prices can go. Although \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  pledged not to turn off the gas or crude oil tap - a decision that could change overnight - his credibility is at rock bottom after the attack on Russia.\par \par \uc2 Gas\par \par \uc2 Even more relevant is the rise in gas, with which Russia covers approximately 25% of EU consumption, a share that in the case of oil exceeds 40%. The reference of the Dutch market TTF has already shot up about 33% compared to Wednesday's close and exceeded 118 euros per megawatt hour (MWh), after touching the barrier of 120 euros/MWh in the first minutes of trading.\par \par \uc2 This is a level that gas has not reached since the eve of last Christmas. In just three days, and since Russia recognized the independence of the Ukrainian provinces of Donetsk and Lugansk, the TTF has risen by more than 60%. The Russian attack on Ukraine comes after Germany decided to indefinitely suspend the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline license and comes at a time when gas reserves in Europe are at a historically low level of just over 30%.\par \par \uc2 The exponential rise in gas prices over the last year is, together with the increase in the price of CO2 emission allowances, mainly responsible for the record electricity prices of the last few months. Each euro increase in the price of this raw material is equivalent to an increase of around two euros in the wholesale electricity market price.\par \par \uc2 Metals\par \par \uc2 The price of gold, the quintessential safe-haven asset, soared 6% this month to over 1,907 dollars per ounce. Following news of the Russian offensive, gold is trading at a record high, soaring 3% to trade at u$s1,969.90, triggering a strong stampede in equities and taking refuge in metals. If tensions continue to rise, analysts believe that the price of the metal is likely to approach record highs when it reached u$s2055.70 due to the fear caused by the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Next, another of the metals rising this morning is silver (+4%), whose price is already above u$s25 per ounce, leaving behind the psychological level of u$s23.80, which it could not break in previous weeks. Therefore, the flight of investors to the metal could trigger the approach to $ 30, a level that it did not visit since last May 2021.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Palladium (+9%) is another metal that is bouncing strongly, trading at a high of u$s2,667.00 and practically approaching levels of August 2021, so we do not rule out a test of u$s2800, the highs of July.\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets\par \par \uc2 The mainstock market indexes of Hong Kong, mainland China and Taiwan plunged after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered a military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong Stock Exchange benchmark, the Hang Seng, fell 3% . The index that measures the performance of mainland Chinese stocks listed on the Hong Kong stock exchange, the Hang Seng China Enterprises, fell as much as 4.14%. For their part, the two main mainland Chinese stock exchanges, Shanghai and Shenzhen, fell by up to 2%.\par \par \uc2 Russian stock market\par \par \uc2 The Moscow Stock Exchange Index fell more than 50% on Thursday and the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble, reached an all-time low against the dollar after Russia launched a military offensive against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The start of the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  was followed by the suspension of trading on the Moscow Stock Exchange and the St. Petersburg Stock Exchange due to strong volatility in the markets. At 10:00 Moscow time, trading was reopened on the Moscow Stock Exchange. Losses were as high as 50%.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds also plunged, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce its first \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  designed to shore up financial stability since 2014, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  annexed Crimea from Ukraine. "In the coming days, the Russian market will be extremely volatile. Given the high risks of tightening sanctions against Russia, we are revising our target benchmarks for Russian stocks," Evgeny Loktyukhov, Head of Economic and Industrial Analysis at PSB, told Russian media outlet Quote.\par \par \uc2 "There has never been such a significant drop in the recent history of the Russian stock market. The main reason for what is happening is the flight from risks amid the escalating geopolitical aspect," added Vladislav Silaev, a senior trader at Alfa Capital. In his opinion, until diplomats and politicians sit down at the negotiating table, the market will remain in the red.\par \par \uc2 The ruble slid to a record low of u$s89.60 against the dollar and approached a crucial threshold of 100 against the euro. It was around 70 to the dollar and 81 to the euro before the recent round of geopolitical tensions between Moscow and the West began to escalate in October.\par \par \uc2 "To stabilize the situation on the financial market, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to initiate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," the bank said Thursday. The move helped the ruble cut losses slightly.\par \par \uc2 The Moscow and St. Petersburg stock exchanges were forced to halt trading after their main indexes plunged by more than 30%. "Trading on all markets was suspended. The restart will be announced at a later date," the Moscow Stock Exchange said in a statement, reports Efe. The St. Petersburg Stock Exchange, the country's second largest, also announced a "ban on bids and contracts for all trading modes and all types of securities.\par \par \uc2 European stock exchanges\par \par \uc2 The main European stock exchanges opened with massive sell-offs by investors. Frankfurt lost 4.9%, Milan 4.3%, Paris 4% and London 2.7%. The Euro Stoxx 50 also opened in the red by more than 3.5%. The \uc2 Ibex\uc2  fell back to close to 8,000 points. The fall is a far cry from the 14.06% of March 12, 2020, when the markets panicked due to the pandemic and the mass confinements that were being decreed halfway around the world. Even so, the tensions unleashed by Putin with major Western powers, including the US and the EU, has dealt a blow to markets that had been heading into negative territory for weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Emerging market equities could fall as much as 10% in the near term unless they can be offset by a mediating policy response, mainly due to investor confidence and growth prospects," said Leonardo Pellandini, an analyst at Julius Baer.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220224ei2o00565\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
