\par\par \b \uc2 Real Time Economics: Russia Attacks Ukraine, Oil Prices Jump, Globalization in Retreat\b0\par\par\uc2 1,257 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2022\par 12:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Russia Attacks Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Russia's invasion of Ukraine heaped fresh risks on a global economy struggling with soaring inflation, supply-chain snarls and a rocky recovery from the pandemic. The economic impact will depend on the scale of the fighting and the new sanctions that the U.S. and its allies have promised. Financial markets reacted swiftly to the invasion, with stock markets in Europe dropping sharply and commodity prices soaring, Paul Hannon reports.\par \par \uc2 A global oil benchmark surged above $100 a barrel for the first time since 2014. While Ukraine isn't a big oil producer, Russia is one of the world's biggest and most influential crude-market players , Joe Wallace and Jenny Strasburg report.\par \par \uc2 Russia is also the single biggest exporter of natural gas, and the country's military push poses another threat to Europe's already tenuous supply . Fresh military action ratchets up risks to Ukraine's gas-transport infrastructure. It also poses the threat that Russia will shut off its gas in response to Western sanctions. More broadly, if European gas prices stay high, that can affect markets further afield, by diverting supplies and driving up prices elsewhere, Joe Wallace reports.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday that it would start \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the ruble plunged to a record low . Russia holds a formidable war chest of over $600 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and gold that it can use in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the ruble, Caitlin Ostroff reports.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and its allies are poised to unveil further sanctions against Russia . WSJ podcast: How Putin has planned for sanctions. Ann Simmons and Georgi Kantchev describe the sanctions that could be coming, and how Russia has prepared for this moment . Western companies with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Russia and Ukraine are girding for disruptions . With \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  freeze, Germany takes first step to cut Russian gas reliance . Crisis kicks off new superpower struggle. Beijing and Moscow now hold a stronger hand in confronting the West than during the Cold War . Live coverage: The latest news on the Russia-Ukraine crisis . What to Watch Today\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  Gov. Andrew Bailey gives opening remarks at a BOE conference on the monetary toolkit at 8:15 a.m. ET.\par \par \uc2 U.S. jobless claims are expected to fall to 235,000 in the week ended Feb. 19 from 248,000 one week earlier. (8:30 a.m. ET)\par \par \uc2 U.S. gross domestic product for the fourth quarter is expected to advance at an annual pace of 7% from the prior quarter, revised from a previous estimate of 6.9% growth. (8:30 a.m. ET)\par \par \uc2 U.S. new-home sales are expected to fall to an annual pace of 803,000 in January from 811,000 one month earlier. (10 a.m. ET)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2 's manufacturing survey is expected to tick up to 25 in February from 24 one month earlier. (11 a.m. ET)\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve speakers: Richmond's \uc2 Thomas Barkin\uc2  to the \uc2 Maryland Chamber of Commerce\uc2  at 9 a.m. ET, San Francisco's \uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  at a \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  panel on unwinding quantitative easing at 11 a.m. ET, Atlanta's \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  at a banking outlook conference at 11:10 a.m. ET, Cleveland's \uc2 Loretta Mester\uc2  on the economic outlook and monetary policy at 12 p.m. ET, and governor Christopher Waller on the economic outlook at 8:25 p.m. ET ( webcast ).\par \par \uc2 Live Q&A: What happens next in Ukraine? Join WSJ's Executive Washington Editor Jerry Seib, in conversation with reporters, as they discuss the latest developments in Ukraine and what might lie ahead . (1 p.m. ET)\par \par \uc2 Enjoying this newsletter? Get more from WSJ and support our work with a special subscription offer .\par \par \uc2 Top Stories Clash Over Ukraine Drives a Dagger Into Globalization\par \par \uc2 The global economic order is unraveling. After the Cold War, globalization was triumphant in Western capitals. Market forces and global rules determined where goods, services, capital and knowledge would flow, not governments, ideology or spheres of influence. Russian president \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  rejected that vision. To him, economic integration ought to reflect geographic, cultural and strategic ties. Ukraine's 2013 bid to integrate with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , and Russia's response-blocking Ukrainian imports and threatening to charge more for natural gas-shows that the growing schism between Russia and the West is as much a fight about the rules that govern the global economy as it is about military alliances and missile installations. To Mr. Putin, the \uc2 EU\uc2  is a threat just like \uc2 NATO\uc2 , Greg Ip writes.\par \par \uc2 Working on the Highway\par \par \uc2 Rising prices and snarled supply chains are poised to blunt the impact of the $1 trillion infrastructure law Congress passed with bipartisan support last year. How many roads, bridges, railways, fiber optic lines and other types of infrastructure the U.S. can build or fix under the law will largely hinge on the extent of increases in everything from the cost of diesel fuel to workers' wages. Elevated costs for materials and labor are already pushing contractors to charge more for construction projects, government data show, increases that economists and industry officials say could reduce the number of infrastructure projects the new federal money can finance. State and local officials facing higher prices may give priority to easier, less ambitious projects, and some worry that a rush of government spending could exacerbate inflation in the industry, Andrew Duehren reports.\par \par \uc2 With spring and summer travel season comes a familiar pandemic reality: Car rentals will be expensive . Rental-car companies and analysts say they expect shortages to continue in the coming months due to the global chip shortage and other supply-chain constraints. With demand for rental cars expected to be high, plan to book early and brace for high prices in popular leisure destinations, Allison Pohle reports.\par \par \uc2 A more infectious type of the Omicron variant has surged to account for more than a third of global Covid-19 cases sequenced recently, adding to the debate about whether countries are ready for full reopening. Health authorities are examining whether the subvariant of Omicron, known as BA.2, could extend the length of Covid-19 waves that have peaked recently in Europe, Japan and some other places, Peter Landers and Miho Inada report.\par \par \uc2 Tweet of the Day What Else We're Reading\par \par \uc2 More than half of unemployed men in their 30s have a criminal record. "We estimate 64% of unemployed men have been arrested, and 46% have been convicted. Unexpectedly, these rates vary only slightly by race and ethnicity. Further investigation of other outcomes such as marriage, education, household net worth, and earnings shows large differences between unemployed men who have a criminal history record and those who do not. One major implication of these findings is that employment services should focus more on the special challenges facing unemployed men with criminal history records," the \uc2 RAND Corporation\uc2 's Shawn Bushway and co-authors write in a new study .\par \par \uc2 About Us\par \par \uc2 Real Time Economics comes to you from WSJ economics reporters and editors around the world. Today's issue was curated and edited by Jeff Sparshott ( @jeffsparshott ) in Manila, James Hookway ( @JamesHookway ) in London, and Greg Ip ( @greg_ip ) in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 This article is a text version of a Wall Street Journal newsletter published earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2022 06:19 ET (11:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220224ei2o001g3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
