\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Deepening Ukraine crisis jolts stocks, sends oil surging\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2022\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * European and U.S. futures tumble\par \par \uc2 * Asia stocks set for worst day in February\par \par \uc2 * Russian rouble hits 15-month low\par \par \uc2 * Most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more muted\par \par \uc2 * Brent crude races to 7-year high, gold jumps\par \par \uc2 By Xie Yu and Alun John\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Feb 22 (Reuters) - Global stocks tumbled while safe-havens rallied and oil surged on Tuesday as Europe's eastern flank stood on the brink of war after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered troops into breakaway regions of eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia Pacific shares outside Japan was on course for its worst day for this month, off 1.66%, weighed by markets in Hong Kong and mainland China. Japan's Nikkei shed 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European markets were also braced for sharp losses at the opening bell, with S&P 500 futures down 1.4%, Nasdaq futures off 1.9%, the pan-region Euro Stoxx 50 futures 1.1% lower, and FTSE futures down 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 Both Asian shares and U.S. and European futures had fallen more earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, Brent crude futures rose 2.1% to $97.44, a new seven-year high, on worries Russia's energy exports could be disrupted. Spot gold added 0.1% to $1,908.10, having earlier hit a new six-month top of $1,913.89.\par \par \uc2 Putin on Monday recognised two breakaway regions in eastern Ukraine as independent and ordered the Russian army to launch what Moscow called a peacekeeping \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the area, upping the ante in a crisis that could unleash a major war.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters witness saw columns of military vehicles including tanks early Tuesday on the outskirts of Donetsk, the capital of one of two breakaway regions, and Putin signed treaties with leaders of the two breakaway regions giving Russia the right to build military bases.\par \par \uc2 Washington and European capitals condemned the move, vowing new sanctions. Ukraine's foreign minister said he had been assured of a "resolute and united" response from the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Following \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest move "we are much closer to military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which of course is going to drive a lot of the risk off sentiment in the markets,\u8221\'20\'1D said Carlos Casanova, senior Asia economist at UBP, adding the short term volatility in markets caused by both geopolitical factors and the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  was 'relentless'.\par \par \uc2 Casanova said the consequences would be higher oil prices, an equity sell off, and people flocking to safe haven assets like the Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, shares of Russian aluminium producer OK \uc2 Rusal\uc2  plunged as much as 22.1% to HK$6.18, their biggest daily percentage decline since April 2018.\par \par \uc2 Away from Russia, and not helping the Hong Kong market, Hong Kong-listed Chinese tech stocks fell 2.3%, with heavyweights Tencent and \uc2 Alibaba\uc2  both hit by speculation about a new wave of regulatory scrutiny.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  QUIETER\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, moves were more muted, barring the Russian rouble which hit an 15-month low early in Asian trading, before steadying.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen walked back early gains which had taken it to a near three-week high of 114.48 per dollar, fellow safe-haven the Swiss franc was holding steady near the previous day's one-month high, and the euro recovered to trade flat after earlier falling to a one-week low of $1.1286.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are not really showing the same level of caution as equity markets," said Matt Simpson, senior market analyst at City Index.\par \par \uc2 "When you read the headlines .. you'd expect to see some follow-through in the markets. We are in equities but we're not in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Interestingly, overnight the Swiss franc was the safe haven, not the Japanese yen."\par \par \uc2 The nerves also drove \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields lower, with benchmark 10-year Treasury yields diving as much as 7 basis points to 1.846%. Bets on Federal Reserve rate hikes also eased and the chance of a 50 basis point hike next month fell below 1-in-5.\par \par \uc2 U.S. policy makers have been sparring publicly about how aggressively to begin tightening.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Governor \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2  said on Monday that she will assess incoming economic data over the next three weeks in deciding whether a half percentage point interest rate rise is needed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next meeting in March.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tom Westbrook in Singapore and Xie Yu in Hong Kong; additional reporting by Alun John in Hong Kong and Andrew Galbraith in Shanghai; editing by Jane Wardell & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-2-22T07:54:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220222ei2m011f9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
