\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks slide, oil jumps as Russia orders troops to Ukraine regions\b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2022\par 03:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * S&P500 and Nasdaq futures tumble, Asia stocks slide over 1.4%\par \par \uc2 * Russian rouble hits 18-month low, Japanese yen up on rush to safety\par \par \uc2 * Brent crude races to 7-month high, gold jumps\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By Tom Westbrook and Xie Yu\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 22 (Reuters) - Global stocks tumbled while safe-havens rallied and oil surged on Tuesday as Europe's eastern flank stood on the brink of war after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered troops into breakaway regions of eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia Pacific shares outside Japan skidded 1.44%, dragged down by markets in Hong Kong and mainland China. Japan's Nikkei shed 2%.\par \par \uc2 S&P 500 futures fell 1.5%, Nasdaq futures lost 2.2%, and the Russian rouble briefly touched an 18-month low in early Asia trade on Tuesday, after Russia's MOEX equity index had fallen 10.5% the day before.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, Brent crude futures rose 2% to $97.21, touching a new seven-year high on worries Russia's energy exports could get disrupted, and spot gold hit a new six-month top of $1,911.56.\par \par \uc2 Putin on Monday recognised two breakaway regions in eastern Ukraine as independent and ordered the Russian army to launch what Moscow called a peacekeeping \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the area, upping the ante in a crisis that could unleash a major war.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters witness saw columns of military vehicles including tanks early Tuesday on the outskirts of Donetsk, the capital of one of two breakaway regions, and Putin signed treaties with leaders of the two breakaway regions giving Russia the right to build military bases.\par \par \uc2 Washington and European capitals condemned the move, vowing new sanctions. Ukraine's foreign minister said he had been assured of a "resolute and united" response from the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, a Biden administration official said Russia's move did not as yet constitute a "further invasion" that would trigger a broader sanctions package, as it was not a departure from what Russia had done already.\par \par \uc2 Following \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest move "we are much closer to military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which of course is going to drive a lot of the risk off sentiment in the markets,\u8221\'20\'1D said Carlos Casanova, senior Asia economist at UBP, adding the short term volatility in markets caused by both geopolitical factors and the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  was 'relentless'.\par \par \uc2 Casanova said the consequences would be higher oil prices, an equity sell off, and people flocking to safe haven assets like the Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, shares of Russian aluminium producer OK \uc2 Rusal\uc2  plunged as much as 22.1% to HK$6.18, their biggest daily percentage decline since April 2018.\par \par \uc2 Away from Russia, and not helping the Hong Kong market, Hong Kong-listed Chinese tech stocks fell 2.25%, with heavyweights Tencent and \uc2 Alibaba\uc2  both hit by speculation about a new wave of regulatory scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen rose as much as 0.2% in Asia to a nearly three-week high of 114.50 per dollar, before paring its gains.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell 0.1% to a one-week low of $1.1296\par \par \uc2 "In these circumstances, risk metrics are the driving force," said NAB head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, \uc2 Ray Attrill\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The nerves also drove \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields lower, with benchmark 10-year Treasury yields diving as much as 5.5 basis points to 1.8715%. Bets on Federal Reserve rate hikes also eased and the chance of a 50-bp hike next month fell below 1-in-5.\par \par \uc2 U.S. policy makers have been sparring publicly about how aggressively to begin tightening.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Governor \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2  said on Monday that she will assess incoming economic data over the next three weeks in deciding whether a half percentage point interest rate rise is needed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next meeting in March.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tom Westbrook in Singapore and Xie Yu in Hong Kong; additional reporting by Alun John in Hong Kong ; editing by Jane Wardell & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-2-22T03:41:08.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220222ei2m00ha9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
