\par\par \b \uc2 European Stocks Drop on Fear of War in Ukraine -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Wallace \par \uc2 1,588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2022\par 15:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Stock futures and bond yields whipsawed, pointing to another volatile day on Wall Street, as investors tried to assess the possibility of a war in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Futures for the S&P 500 rose 0.1% after sliding early Monday. Contracts for the technology-focused Nasdaq-100 and the Dow Jones Industrial Average also ticked up 0.1%, recouping earlier losses. Stocks fell Friday after U.S. warnings that Moscow could invade Ukraine at any moment.\par \par \uc2 Yields on government bonds also bounced back, after falling as investors reached for the safety of U.S. Treasurys. The yield on 10-year Treasury notes ticked up to 1.968% from 1.951% Friday. Bond yields and prices move in opposite directions.\par \par \uc2 Overseas stock markets dropped, catching up with Wall Street's late-week tumble. The Stoxx Europe 600 lost 1.9%, led lower by banks and travel and leisure companies. Japan's Nikkei 225 fell 2.2% and China's Shanghai Composite Index fell 1%.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices edged down, after jumping on concern a war would curtail supplies of Russian crude amid a lack significant spare supplies. Brent, the benchmark in energy markets, fell 0.8% to $93.72 a barrel, holding near its highest level since 2014.\par \par \uc2 Prices for natural gas -- of which Russia is the single biggest exporter globally -- rose on both sides of the Atlantic. In the U.S., gas prices rose 3.1% to $4.06 per million British thermal units. Prices in Europe, which depends on Russia for much of its gas, a chunk of it flowing through Ukraine, jumped 5%. Prices for palladium -- a metal widely used in the auto industry and heavily produced in Russia -- added 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 Stocks have been buffeted this year by the prospect of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raising interest rates. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is gearing up to increase borrowing costs to combat the highest rate of inflation in four decades, winding down the easy-money policies that have pushed riskier assets higher for much of the past two years.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of a ground war in Europe has loomed as an additional source of uncertainty for investors. Moscow has denied intending to invade Ukraine, but Russia's military buildup has quickened, with forces positioned on three sides of the country.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, investors had wagered that war would be averted, piling into the ruble and hryvnia, as well as Russian and Ukrainian government bonds. Those assets were volatile Monday, initially selling off before clawing back some ground.\par \par \uc2 Russia's benchmark stock index, the Moex Russia, fell 1%. Ukraine's hryvnia weakened 1.7% to 28.55 a dollar. The yield on a dollar-denominated Russian government bond maturing in 2026 rose to 4.441% from 3.926% Friday, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 . A Ukrainian dollar bond maturing in 2033 traded at a yield of 10.798%, up from 10.222%.\par \par \uc2 Catching some investors off guard, the ruble strengthened 1.1% to 76.63 a dollar. Timothy Ash, a strategist at \uc2 BlueBay Asset Management\uc2 , said the move suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. "On the geopolitical risk, the ruble should be weaker," he added.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has ample reserves of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with which to support the ruble. The bank didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Investors say the standoff over Ukraine is difficult to trade. If Moscow were to attack and the U.S. and its allies responded with sanctions, the hostilities could affect the world economy and markets in unpredictable ways.\par \par \uc2 One likely consequence, given Russia's position as a commodities superpower, would be higher energy prices, which could keep up the pressure on central banks to raise rates. At least in the short term, stocks and bond yields would likely decline as investors sought safe assets, investors say.\par \par \uc2 "We have the inflation story and then we have the Russian story," said Lars Skovgaard Andersen, senior investment strategist at Danske Bank Wealth Management. In the event of an invasion, "there will be some negative effect on markets, but I also think investors are incorporating this," he added.\par \par \uc2 Among individual U.S. stocks, Splunk rose 10% in premarket trading after The Wall Street Journal reported that \uc2 Cisco Systems\uc2  had made a takeover offer worth more than $20 billion for the software maker.\par \par \uc2 Shares of airlines were hammered in Europe after reports that several were avoiding Ukraine's airspace. Budget carrier \uc2 Wizz Air\uc2  dropped 7.6%, \uc2 British Airways\uc2  owner \uc2 International Consolidated Airlines Group\uc2  lost 5% and \uc2 Deutsche Lufthansa\uc2  fell 3.4%.\par \par \uc2 -- Anna Hirtenstein and Caitlin Ostroff contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Joe Wallace at joe.wallace@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 14 Feb 2022 09:36 ET  U.S. Stocks Fall, Bond Yields Swing on Fear of Ukraine War -- WSJ\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Joe Wallace \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks opened lower Monday, as investors tried to assess the possibility of a war in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 99 points, or 0.2%, shortly after the opening bell. The S&P 500 also dropped 0.2% and the Nasdaq Composite declined 0.3%. Stocks fell Friday after U.S. warnings that Moscow could invade Ukraine at any moment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields on government bonds bounced back, after falling as investors reached for the safety of U.S. Treasurys. The yield on 10-year Treasury notes ticked up to 1.982% from 1.951% Friday. Bond yields and prices move in opposite directions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overseas stock markets dropped, catching up with Wall Street's late-week tumble. The Stoxx Europe 600 lost 1.7%, led lower by banks and travel and leisure companies. Japan's Nikkei 225 fell 2.2% and China's Shanghai Composite Index fell 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices edged down, after jumping on concern a war would curtail supplies of Russian crude amid a lack significant spare supplies. Brent, the benchmark in energy markets, fell 0.5% to $93.89 a barrel, holding near its highest level since 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices for natural gas -- of which Russia is the single biggest exporter globally -- rose on both sides of the Atlantic. In the U.S., gas prices rose 3.9% to $4.09 per million British thermal units. Prices in Europe, which depends on Russia for much of its gas, a chunk of it flowing through Ukraine, jumped about 5%. Prices for palladium -- a metal widely used in the auto industry and heavily produced in Russia -- added around 3.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks have been buffeted this year by the prospect of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  raising interest rates. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is gearing up to increase borrowing costs to combat the highest rate of inflation in four decades, winding down the easy-money policies that have pushed riskier assets higher for much of the past two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The possibility of a ground war in Europe has loomed as an additional source of uncertainty for investors. Moscow has denied intending to invade Ukraine, but Russia's military buildup has quickened, with forces positioned on three sides of the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent weeks, investors had wagered that war would be averted, piling into the ruble and hryvnia, as well as Russian and Ukrainian government bonds. Those assets were volatile Monday, initially selling off before clawing back some ground.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's benchmark stock index, the Moex Russia, fell 1%. Ukraine's hryvnia weakened 1.7% to 28.55 a dollar. The yield on a dollar-denominated Russian government bond maturing in 2026 rose to 4.441% from 3.926% Friday, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tradeweb\f28 \uc2 \f2 . A Ukrainian dollar bond maturing in 2033 traded at a yield of 10.798%, up from 10.222%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Catching some investors off guard, the ruble strengthened 1.1% to 76.63 a dollar. Timothy Ash, a strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BlueBay Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said the move suggested \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. "On the geopolitical risk, the ruble should be weaker," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has ample reserves of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with which to support the ruble. The bank didn't respond to a request for comment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors say the standoff over Ukraine is difficult to trade. If Moscow were to attack and the U.S. and its allies responded with sanctions, the hostilities could affect the world economy and markets in unpredictable ways.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One likely consequence, given Russia's position as a commodities superpower, would be higher energy prices, which could keep up the pressure on central banks to raise rates. At least in the short term, stocks and bond yields would likely decline as investors sought safe assets, investors say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have the inflation story and then we have the Russian story," said Lars Skovgaard Andersen, senior investment strategist at Danske Bank Wealth Management. In the event of an invasion, "there will be some negative effect on markets, but I also think investors are incorporating this," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among individual U.S. stocks, Splunk rose 4.5% after The Wall Street Journal reported that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cisco Systems\f28 \uc2 \f2  had made a takeover offer worth more than $20 billion for the software maker.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of airlines were hammered in Europe after reports that several were avoiding Ukraine's airspace. Budget carrier \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wizz Air\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 7.4%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 British Airways\f28 \uc2 \f2  owner \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Consolidated Airlines Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  lost 5% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Lufthansa\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 3.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Anna Hirtenstein and Caitlin Ostroff contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Joe Wallace at joe.wallace@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 14, 2022 09:36 ET (14:36 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220214ei2e003fb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
