\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Ilya Ponomariov: 'With the Ukrainian crisis Putin's aim is to control Belarus'.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In 2014, when \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  asked for the formal approval of the Duma (the Russian Parliament) to complete the annexation of Crimea only one deputy voted against. It was Ilya Ponomariov (Moscow, 1975), who years later had to leave Russia. Today he lives in Kiev, where he combines business and public activity. In his opinion, the Kremlin's goal is not Ukraine, but to control Belarus and to be accepted by the West.\par \par \uc2 Will it be possible to solve the current confrontation through diplomacy or should Ukraine really prepare for a Russian attack?\par \par \uc2 I am convinced that now there is not going to be any new attack from Russia, and that it has not planned it. I think it is a game between the United States and Russia. Each side has its goals and they demand from the other that they be met at the expense of Ukraine. The Biden administration needs a brilliant victory after what happened in Afghanistan and on the eve of this year's Congressional elections. It is in their interest to have maximum tension here and then heroically defend Ukraine. On the other hand, the Kremlin has everything ready to de facto control Belarus. Not in the form of annexation, as with Crimea, but with a state union between the two countries. And it is in Russia's interest for this to happen without sanctions. For that it needs to make a trade-off: we will not attack Ukraine, but do not pay attention to Belarus.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has regained the Western support front again, but its economy is suffering." Are you saying that Ukraine is a kind of victim of this game?\par \par \uc2 Ukraine also gains benefits, because the support front that was formed in 2014 and 2015 had virtually ceased to exist last year, and now it has been restored. But it also suffers great harm, because rumors of attacks have greatly weakened business activity in the country. A large number of businessmen have stopped investing, the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened. That is why Zelensky and his team are trying to calm things down and say that there will be no attack.\par \par \uc2 Why is the Kremlin now putting pressure on the West and Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 A year ago now Russia already put large numbers of troops near the Ukrainian border, and then in the West there was no fuss like there is now. It was quieter, although there was a large Russian military deployment. And all that ended with a personal meeting between Putin and Biden (in June 2021 in Geneva) that the Kremlin had long been looking for. And from that experience they learned that you have to tense the situation, because the method works. So they have now repeated something that worked for them a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Putin wants to show the threat of an attack, but not execute it." The Kremlin provides no clue as to what it will do if the West does not agree to its demands. Why is Putin so cryptic?\par \par \uc2 He is a Chekist, his profession is to create intrigue. That is why one should not expect direct confrontation from Putin. He conducts special \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , secret, under the carpet, as he did in the Donbass, when he was declaring that they were not there. And that he is openly showing his troops near the Ukrainian border shows that what he wants is to show a threat, but not to execute it.\par \par \uc2 Putin has convinced himself that with the West there are no clear agreements and then it is difficult to keep them. Could Putin simply be seeking respect from the West, a return to international forums like the G-8?\par \par \uc2 He doesn't believe in that. He has convinced himself that with the West there are no clear agreements and then it is difficult to keep them. So he needs a tactical victory to be implemented here and now. That short-term goal, in my view, is Belarus.\par \par \uc2 Can Western sanctions change Russia's strategy?\par \par \uc2 No. But that doesn't mean they won't suffer from them. But they are not enough because they were made inconsistently and belatedly. A simple example: the story of excluding Russia from the SWIFT system. If this measure had been implemented in 2014 then for Russian business it would have had the effect of a bomb. But if such a measure is taken now, it will most likely have a boomerang effect, because during these seven years the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has thoroughly prepared itself against such measures.\par \par \uc2 Is there a chance that Ukraine will regain Crimea in the future, or has Putin left it securely tied to Russia?\par \par \uc2 As long as Putin is in power the status of Crimea will not change. After that it will depend on how the political scenario in Russia transforms. Events may unfold in a variety of ways. Including the division of Russia. I hope this will not happen, but anything can happen. Putin has built a system that now stands on his person. And no doubt the Crimea issue will return that day to the agenda. But as long as Putin is in power, for him this is a fundamental, immovable issue.\par \par \uc2 If the conflict in the Donbass were somehow resolved, could Ukraine recognize that Crimea is already part of Russia?\par \par \uc2 It is not possible. I think that if it were put to the people of Ukraine to recognize Crimea as Russian in exchange for peace in the Donbass, I would say no. Ukrainian society is ready to make many and varied compromises to achieve peace in Donbass, even more than its politicians. But not at the cost of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 In Crimea it is necessary to respond with something to the fake referendum organized by the Kremlin." When you voted against Russia's annexation of Crimea, your argument was that the desire to become independent or not from Ukraine was an internal issue and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Is it possible to hold a referendum in Crimea in the future to put an end to the issue?\par \par \uc2 At some point we will have to go through some referendum in Crimea, because it is necessary to respond with something to the fake referendum that the Kremlin organized. And that can only be done with a real referendum. Now this idea is not popular in Ukraine. But I think that at some point you will be able to come to an agreement. To do this, it is necessary for the Russian army to leave, to vote only the people who then lived in Crimea, and not the people who have come from Russia, mostly military. It would be necessary to conduct a free election campaign, as in Scotland, where each force set out its position and as a result take in the end an objective and fair decision.\par \par \uc2 How do you see the Russian extra-parliamentary opposition after the events of last year, after the imprisonment of Navalny?\par \par \uc2 The so-called Russian non-systemic opposition is defeated, there is no place for it at the moment. I am not saying that there are no concrete opponents, who are increasingly appearing in the West, but there is no organized political force. From my point of view, with his return from Germany, Navalni unintentionally helped the power to achieve an excuse to defeat it.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220211ei2b001y0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw94\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1410\pichgoal600
