\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Rtgs: Severe Russia Sanctions Could Pressure Sovereign, Bank Ratings\b0\par\par\uc2 2,046 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2022\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-08 February 2022: This commentary was originally published at 6:19 AM ET today and is being republished for technical reasons.\par \par \uc2 Severe sanctions on Russia could have negative rating implications for the sovereign and banks, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says. For the sovereign, this would depend on their capacity to undermine macroeconomic and financial stability, or impede sovereign debt service payments, in line with \uc2 Fitch\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s rating sensitivities. For banks, sanctions that impede the ability to execute foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) payments could be ratings negative.\par \par \uc2 Our base case is that new sanctions would not be sufficiently severe to warrant negative rating actions, but the risk of this scenario has become more significant in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18BBB\u8217\'20\'19/Stable rating is constrained by existing sanctions and the prospect of additional sanctions weighing further on GDP growth, financing flexibility and prospects for structural improvements, which are credit weaknesses relative to peers. The probability of some form of new sanctions was high when we affirmed the rating in December.\par \par \uc2 Tensions over Ukraine have increased the risk of conflict and more far-reaching sanctions, although our base case remains there is no full-scale invasion. Sanctions would likely be calibrated according to the extent of any Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is highly uncertain.\par \par \uc2 Triggers could include contained military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or cyber-attacks. In the absence of conflict, more likely sanctions include preventing US investors transacting in secondary market sovereign debt, and additional sanctions on senior officials, government institutions and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supporters\u8217\'20\'19 wealth. Alone, these would be unlikely to cause ratings pressure.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s strengthened macro-policy framework since 2015, the build-up of large external and fiscal buffers and reduced external exposures have enhanced resilience to shocks.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/537f37d0-34cc-474c-a0d2-4243af70dcc1?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FW Russian Improved Resilience Feb 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 US secondary market sovereign debt sanctions would further constrain Russia\u8217\'20\'19s fiscal financing flexibility and foreign investment, while capital outflows and a weaker Russian rouble could precipitate tighter policy. However, this would not significantly affect macro-stability or buffers, which include a Federal Treasury cash reserve and National Wealth Fund assets totaling 16.5% of GDP, and the \u8216\'20\'18BBB\u8217\'20\'19 category\u8217\'20\'19s highest international reserves (17 months of CXP).\par \par \uc2 Non-resident OFZ holdings have fallen to 20.5% of the total (below 3% of GDP); we forecast general government surpluses (average 1.3% of GDP in 2022-2023) and the banking sector can absorb more debt. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  could provide additional liquidity and address market volatility through \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/04dfc0d4-6db1-463c-843f-426a8c48f886?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FW \b  \highlight22Russia\b0 \highlight22  Reserves, NWF Assets Feb 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, heightened risks around Ukraine and the US draft Menendez Bill underline the potential in a downside scenario for measures with a larger sovereign credit impact, such as preventing systemic banks and corporates from transacting in US dollars or accessing the international payments system, or broad-based energy market sanctions disrupting exports.\par \par \uc2 The biggest impact on banking sector creditworthiness would be if sanctions impaired large state-owned banks\u8217\'20\'19 ability to execute FC payments, particularly through banning dollar transactions. Exclusion from SWIFT\u8217\'20\'19s messaging system would also cause sizeable near-term disruption, given alternative systems have limited international participation. We do not expect such measures in our base case, partly because they would impede repayment of foreign creditors, and consider sanctions on less systemically important banks more likely.\par \par \uc2 The rating impact on banks of such severe sanctions would depend on their formulation, especially how far payments to creditors could be channelled through alternative payment systems and other Russian banks, and exposures switched to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or settled with Asian and European counterparties. Bank ratings would likely be placed on Rating Watch Negative if such sanctions were imposed, and could be downgraded if greater uncertainty over the ability and willingness to service debt persisted. If sanctions resulted in freezing banks' FC assets, Viability Ratings would also come under pressure.\par \par \uc2 The impact on financial metrics is unlikely to result in downgrades given banks have generally large liquidity buffers to offset deposit outflows, foreign payment schedules are not onerous (reflecting adjustment to earlier sanctions) and potential regulatory forbearance, if needed.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/a6606044-4087-49f2-b59c-b6bf9ea9979e?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FW Russian Bank FC Exposure Feb 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Rated Russian corporates are generally well positioned to cope with sanctions. Balance sheets are strong, including FC positions, and corporates with FC external debt typically benefit from export receipts. The biggest impact would be if sanctions restricted corporates\u8217\'20\'19 ability to access FC cash or draw credit lines, and depends on whether Russian banks could still transact in dollars and international banks could service export cash flow and facilitate FC cash accumulation. Lower-rated Eurobond corporate issuers with short-term maturities are most sensitive to such measures.\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Peter Fitzpatrick, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 1103, Email: peter.fitzpatrick@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 . All opinions expressed are those of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  (\uc2 Fitch\uc2 ) credit ratings are subject to certain limitations and disclaimers. Please read these limitations and disclaimers by following this link: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/understandingcreditratings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/understandingcreditratings}}}\uc2 . In addition, the following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/rating-definitions-document"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/rating-definitions-document}}}\uc2  details \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's rating definitions for each rating scale and rating categories, including definitions relating to default. Published ratings, criteria, and methodologies are available from this site at all times. \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's code of conduct, confidentiality, conflicts of interest, affiliate firewall, compliance, and other relevant policies and procedures