\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Russia tensions hobble Ukraine's fight against inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2022\par 14:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * CPI poll data reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/econ-polls?RIC=UACPIY%3DECI\par \par \uc2 * CPI full year poll data reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/econ-polls?RIC=U reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/econ-polls?RIC=UACPIAPACPIAP\par \par \uc2 * Hryvnia rate poll data reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/\b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2 -polls?RIC=UAH=\par \par \uc2 By Natalia Zinets\par \par \uc2 KYIV, Feb 8 (Reuters) - Ukraine inflation slowed down only slightly to 9.9% in January as worries about a Russian military attack dragged down the hryvnia and weighed on prices despite another interest rate increase, a Reuters poll showed on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Russia, which seized Crimea from Ukraine in 2014 and backs separatists in the east of the country, has massed more than 100,000 troops near the Ukrainian border in recent weeks. Moscow denies planning a further invasion.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian analysts from banks and brokerages estimated January inflation at 9.9%, according to the median figure in the poll, compared with 10% in December.\par \par \uc2 "We expect it to decline slightly in January," said Serhii Kolodii from the Ukrainian subsidiary of \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "A particular risk for inflation is represented by the devaluation of the hryvnia in the second half of January, mainly caused by geopolitical tension," Kolodii said.\par \par \uc2 The hryvnia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost about 5% against U.S. dollar in January by slipping to 29/$1 for the first time since February 2015. This reduced the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to restrain price rises.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2  (NBU) raised its key interest rate by a further one percentage point to 10% in January following five increases in 2021 to fight inflation, which peaked at a three-year-high of 11% in September.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, analysts expect no further weakening of the hryvnia, which regained some strength in early February and returned to the level of around 28/$1. The median forecast in the poll shows the hryvnia at 27.8/$1 at the end of February.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's less aggressive rhetoric and significant NBU \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  played in favour of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," analysts from the ICU brokerage said in a report. (Editing by Matthias Williams and Jane Merriman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-2-8T14:24:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220208ei2802te3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
