\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Russian rouble recovers after hitting 5-month low on geopolitical concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2021\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds detail)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, April 8 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble recovered slightly on Thursday, holding near the 77-mark against the dollar, after hitting a five-month low in the previous session amid renewed tensions between Moscow and the West over Ukraine and other issues.\par \par \uc2 Largely ignoring a decline in the price of oil, Russia's key export, the rouble was 0.2% stronger at 76.80 against the dollar at 0702 GMT, having hit on Wednesday its weakest level since Nov. 5 of 78.0450.\par \par \uc2 The dollar pinned near two-week troughs versus its major peers on Thursday, tracking Treasury yields lower, after minutes of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's March policy meeting offered no new catalyst to dictate market direction.\par \par \uc2 Investors are now awaiting a speech by Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  at a virtual \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  conference later on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The rouble was also up 0.2% versus the euro at 91.24 . It remains far from levels of around 70 seen in early-2020 before the COVID-19 pandemic went global and oil prices crashed.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil, a global benchmark for Russia\u8217\'20\'19s main export, fell 0.5% at $62.85 a barrel after official data showed a big increase in U.S. gasoline stocks.\par \par \uc2 The rouble has recently taken a hit from increased geopolitical concerns as Russia began a planned inspection of its army's combat readiness, while \uc2 NATO\uc2  voiced concerns about a Russian military build-up near eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Russia said a serious escalation in the conflict in eastern Ukraine's Donbass region could "destroy" Ukraine. However the Kremlin has also said its military movements pose no threat to Ukraine or anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 The threat of further Western sanctions against Russia has pressured its assets for months, especially after U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in March that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  would "pay a price" for meddling in U.S. elections and cyber-hacking, allegations that Moscow denies.\par \par \uc2 However, the rouble drew support from comments on Wednesday by \uc2 Nikolai Patrushev\uc2 , secretary of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Security Council\uc2 , that Moscow had no plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eastern Ukraine, said Alor Broker analyst Alexei Antonov, who added that he expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen moderately further.\par \par \uc2 The rouble may also see some support later this month from a possible decision by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates as inflation is showing few signs of slowing.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will consider holding or raising interest rates by either 25 or 50 basis points at its April 23 board meeting, the bank's head of monetary policy told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Russian stocks firmed on Thursday. The dollar-denominated RTS index rose 1% at 1,443.5. The rouble-based MOEX Russian index climbed 0.5% at 3,519.2.\u8203\'20\'0B\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Andrey Ostroukh, additional reporting by Anna Rzhevkina; editing by Uttaresh.V and Gareth Jones) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-4-8T08:44:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210408eh48014fl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
