\par\par \b \uc2 EXPLAINER-What if U.S. sanctions target Russia's OFZ government bonds?\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 13:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW/LONDON, March 31 (Reuters) - U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's recent warning to Russia's \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  against election meddling and cyber hacks is raising expectations of new, more biting sanctions on Russia's financial markets.\par \par \uc2 These could range from freezing the U.S. assets of people close to Putin, all the way to curbing the Kremlin's ability to issue debt.\par \par \uc2 Part of that latter option could be barring U.S. investors from Russia's rouble-denominated debt, as they already are from buying Moscow's dollar-denominated bonds. Here are some of the possible consequences:\par \par \uc2 RUSSIAN TREASURY BOND BAN?\par \par \uc2 U.S. investors were banned from buying new dollar-denominated Russian debt in 2014 after the Kremlin's annexation of the Crimea region from Ukraine. Now rouble-denominated debt, known by their Russian abbreviation OFZ, could be added to that list, analysts have suggested.\par \par \uc2 Russia uses OFZs to finance its budget deficit. Total OFZ issuance is 13.86 trillion roubles ($182.22 billion), \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data show. Foreign pension funds and other international asset managers hold just over 3 trillion roubles worth, roughly 23%.\par \par \uc2 That share is the lowest it has been in five years. A year ago, it was nearly 35%. Russian banks instead are plugging the gap, last month buying over 40% of the debt issued by the government.\par \par \uc2 U.S. funds would be allowed to keep existing OFZ holdings, so only newly issued debt would be affected, although even that could be complicated by the fact Russia often adds to, or "taps", existing bonds rather than issue new ones altogether.\par \par \uc2 Russia expects to issue 3.7 trillion roubles ($48.65 billion) of OFZs this year, so how much would it matter if U.S. investors were no longer buyers?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data shows nearly 7% of OFZs are held by U.S. investors. Extrapolated to new sales, that would take away almost $3.4 billion in demand.\par \par \uc2 If the UK joined the ban, that could be another 7% of demand gone. The \uc2 EU\uc2  would be another 5%, although there is little suggestion it would participate.\par \par \uc2 CAN RUSSIA ABSORB SANCTIONS ON OFZ?\par \par \uc2 Russia's finance ministry\uc2  has downplayed the likelihood of Biden sanctioning OFZ issuance but said it would be prepared for such a move. .\par \par \uc2 Last week, it cancelled weekly OFZ bond auctions for the first time since August after 10-year benchmark yields - a proxy for borrowing costs - jumped to 7.36% in the secondary market, the highest in almost a year.\par \par \uc2 VTB and \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 , Russia's two largest state-run banks, have said they would be ready to increase their OFZ holdings. VTB Chief Executive \uc2 Andrey Kostin\uc2  reckons that with Russia\u8217\'20\'19s ratio of public debt to gross domestic product still only around 20%, the government could double its debt to around 30 trillion roubles without much trouble.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  holds over 3 trillion roubles worth of OFZs and VTB has over 900 billion roubles worth. Some other banks, such as Otkritie, also hold significant amounts.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , officials have said.\par \par \uc2 Finally, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's budget could get a 2 trillion-rouble boost from higher oil prices. That should mean Moscow would eventually need to borrow less. The 3.7 trillion-rouble borrowing plan could be cut by as much as 700 billion, it has said. .\par \par \uc2 WIDER IMPLICATIONS\par \par \uc2 Could sanctions on OFZ cause knock-on effects on big global bond indexes run by the likes of \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  and Bloomberg-\uc2 Barclays\uc2 ? Those indexes are tracked by funds managing trillions of dollars worldwide and the ejection of a country can have major ramifications.\par \par \uc2 It looks unlikely; after all Russian dollar debt wasn't ejected from any indexes, despite being under sanction since 2014.\par \par \uc2 But ejection can also come down to what investors call "liquidity issues", essentially whether there's enough trading volumes in a particular market.\par \par \uc2 And Russia's share in \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's GBI-EM index, the main global benchmark for emerging-market local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, has been dropping over the past year.\par \par \uc2 It sits now at a still-substantial 7%, but it's down from 8.9% a year ago. Before the Ukraine crisis of 2014, Russia's weight was 10%, the maximum allowed for a single country. ($1 = 76.0600 roubles)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Katya Golubkova in Moscow and Marc Jones in London; editing by Larry King Editing by) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-3-31T12:48:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210331eh3v02bke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
