\par\par \b \uc2 Rouble steady on rise in oil prices, Russian coronavirus cases\b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2020\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, May 12 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble was steady in early trade on Tuesday, as the market digested higher oil prices, a decrease in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rising number of cases of the novel coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 On the first working day of the week following a public holiday, the rouble was flat at 73.42 versus the dollar at 0750 GMT and firmed 0.2% to 79.83 versus the euro .\par \par \uc2 The rouble retained support from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales, which, however, declined to the equivalent of 14.8 billion roubles ($201 million) last week from levels of more than 20 billion roubles a few days earlier.\par \par \uc2 The state \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports with a two-day lag, are pegged to prices for oil, an export vital to Russia's economy. Global benchmark Brent crude futures were 0.9% higher at $29.88 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 The rouble may see some support from demand for Russian OFZ treasury bonds after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will consider cutting the key rate by 100 basis points next month.\par \par \uc2 Rate cuts push OFZ yields lower, which inversely move their prices higher, meaning investors may want to buy into the papers before their prices go up.\par \par \uc2 ITI Capital said it sees room for a 70-100 basis points decline in OFZ yields, which suggests their price can increase by up to 10%.\par \par \uc2 The novel coronavirus outbreak remains in focus. Russia on Tuesday reported 10,899 new cases over a 24 hour period, bringing the nationwide total past that of Britain to 232,243, the third-highest worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Despite the increase, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on Monday announced a gradual easing of coronavirus lockdown measures.\par \par \uc2 The lifting of restrictions in Russia would be gradual and each region would need to tailor its approach to varying local conditions. Moscow has said it will keep its own lockdown measures in place until May 31.\par \par \uc2 Russian stock indexes were down.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated RTS index declined 0.4% to 1,131.4 points. The rouble-based MOEX Russian index was 0.2% lower at 2,637.6 points.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the currently firm oil market, we expect a moderate decline in the MOEX in the first half of the day and see risks of its decline towards the lower boundary of the 2,600-2,700 range by the end of the day," Bogdan Zvarich, chief analyst at \uc2 Promsvyazbank\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see (Reporting by Andrey Ostroukh; editing by Barbara Lewis) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-5-12T09:11:06.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200512eg5c018zi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
