\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Rouble weighed down by tariff war worries, absence of  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2020\par 18:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, May 1 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble slipped on Friday in thin interbank trading, weighed down by worries of a renewed tariff war between China and the United States and the absence of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a local market holiday.\par \par \uc2 At 1600 GMT, the rouble was 1.6% weaker against the dollar at 75.48 and had lost 2.0% to trade at 83.01 versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 Risk sentiment on global markets soured after U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threatened to impose new tariffs on China over the coronavirus crisis. His comments pressured stocks and sparked a phase of risk-off positioning.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began buttressing the rouble in March by selling increasing amounts of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a daily basis. The absence of such sales on Friday because of a public holiday put further pressure on the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bank, which reports its sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a two-day lag, said on Thursday that it had sold 20.4 billion roubles ($270 million) worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday this week.\par \par \uc2 Russia only has a handful of working days between May 1 and May 11 this year, with back-to-back long weekends for the Labour Day and Victory Day state holidays.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy has been pummelled by low oil prices and the coronavirus pandemic, which has so far killed 1,169 people in the country.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said the economy could contract by as much as 8% in the second quarter, while the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) has said the Russian economy could contract by 5.5% this year.\par \par \uc2 The economy ministry said on Thursday that growth in Russia's gross domestic product had fallen to 0.9% in year-on-year terms in March, from 2.8% in February.\par \par \uc2 On Friday prices for Brent crude oil, a global benchmark for Russia's main export, were down 1.3% at $26.13 a barrel by 1609 GMT.\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see ($1 = 75.3221 roubles) (Reporting by Gabrielle T\u233\'00\'E9trault-Farber; Editing by Alexander Smith and Nick Tattersall) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-5-1T17:41:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200501eg5102cc5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
