\par\par \b \uc2 Rouble slips in absence of  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\uc2  on public holiday\b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2020\par 15:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, May 1 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble slipped on Friday in thin interbank trading in the absence of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the local market was closed for a public holiday.\par \par \uc2 At 1315 GMT, the rouble was 1.4% weaker against the dollar at 75.43 and had lost 1.6% to trade at 82.69 versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 In March, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began supporting the rouble by selling increasing amounts of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 The bank, which reports its sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a two-day lag, said on Thursday that it had sold 20.4 billion roubles ($270.84 million) worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday this week.\par \par \uc2 Russia only has a handful of working days between May 1 and May 11 this year, with back-to-back long weekends for the Labour Day and Victory Day state holidays.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy has been pummelled by low oil prices and the coronavirus pandemic, which has so far killed 1,169 people in the country.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said the economy could contract by as much as 8% in the second quarter, while the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) has said the Russian economy could contract by 5.5% this year.\par \par \uc2 The economy ministry said on Thursday that growth in Russia's gross domestic product had fallen to 0.9% in year-on-year terms in March, from 2.8% in February.\par \par \uc2 On Friday prices for Brent crude oil, a global benchmark for Russia's main export, were up 1.8% at $26.95 a barrel by 1326 GMT.\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see ($1 = 75.3221 roubles) (Reporting by Gabrielle T\u233\'00\'E9trault-Farber; Editing by Alexander Smith) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-5-1T14:46:24.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200501eg5101tif\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
