\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Rouble firms after rate cut, shrugs off drop in oil prices\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2020\par 15:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, April 27 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble firmed slightly on Monday, supported by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to cut interest rates and by its daily sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which outweighed another drop in prices for oil, Russia's key export.\par \par \uc2 At 1328 GMT, the rouble was 0.1% stronger against the dollar at 74.45 and had eased 0.2% to 80.81 versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 Low and volatile oil prices kept putting pressure on the rouble, along with the global economic crisis stemming from the novel coronavirus outbreak.\par \par \uc2 "We expect high volatility on the Russian market and oil prices to move within a wide range this week," BCS brokerage said.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil, a global benchmark for Russia's main export, was down 8.3% at $19.66 a barrel, resuming its slide on concerns over scarce storage capacity, especially in the United States, and global economic doldrums from the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In Russia, the economy is set to shrink by up to 6% this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new forecasts showed.\par \par \uc2 But the rouble could gain this week after the rate cut and the expectations for lower rates in the future, which should support demand for Russian bonds, said Tatiana Evdokimova, chief economist at Nordea Bank in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also supporting the rouble with daily sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from state reserves, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2015.\par \par \uc2 The rouble gained last week after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates in an effort to counter the economic crisis triggered by the coronavirus epidemic and signalled more cuts later this year.\par \par \uc2 Lower \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates drive bond yields lower while pushing their prices higher. This makes Russia's OFZ treasury bonds more attractive for investors who bet on further rate cuts by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yields on 10-year OFZ bonds slid to 6.06%, levels last seen in early March before a global sell-off in emerging-market assets.\par \par \uc2 "This week, trading activity in the OFZ market will be below average - a long May holiday weekend starts in Russia on Friday," \uc2 VTB Capital\uc2  said. The Moscow Exchange will be closed on May 1 and will partly re-open on May 4.\par \par \uc2 Russian stock indexes were slightly up, shrugging off yet another decline in crude prices, as they priced in what \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  said was the mood of optimism on global markets as some governments in Europe prepare to ease lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated RTS index was up 0.3% to 1,084.6 points. The rouble-based MOEX Russian index was 0.1% higher at 2,563.1 points.\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see (Reporting by Andrey Ostroukh, editing by Larry King and Ken Ferris) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-4-27T14:42:43.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200427eg4r02gqj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
