\par\par \b \uc2 Rouble weakens again as oil prices collapse; OFZ auctions in focus\b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2020\par 09:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, April 22 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble weakened early on Wednesday as prices for oil, Russia's main exports, collapsed because of shrinking demand for everything from gasoline to jet fuel.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Russian finance ministry\uc2  is in focus as it plan to hold auctions of OFZ treasury bonds, demand for which is usually seen as a gauge of investors' sentiment towards Russian assets.\par \par \uc2 At 0714 GMT, the rouble was 0.2% weaker against the dollar at 77.24 and had lost 0.2% to trade at 83.90 versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 The rouble posted losses for the fourth day in a row, heading towards the four-year lows it touched a month ago, hit by from lower oil prices and the coronavirus outbreak that's pushing the Russian economy into recession.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil, a global benchmark for Russia's main export, fell to its lowest since 1999 as worldwide supply looks set to overwhelm demand for months to come and current production cuts fall short of offsetting that glut.\par \par \uc2 Brent futures were down 13.5% at $16.71 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 The rouble, however, saw some support from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which kept increasing its daily sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from state reserves, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2015.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 16.8 billion roubles ($217.36 million) worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up from 14.8 billion on Friday and 12.9 billion on Monday last week, its data showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Export-focused companies can offset some losses in the rouble as they need to convert part of their \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues to meet local month-end taxes.\par \par \uc2 Russian stock indexes were surprisingly stronger on Wednesday, shrugging off the sell-off on the oil market.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated RTS index was up 0.9% to 1,020.7 points. The rouble-based MOEX Russian index was 0.6% higher at 2,501.5 points.\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 77.2900 roubles) (Reporting by Andrey Ostroukh, editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-4-22T08:26:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200422eg4m014nx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
